QB.add(13,[{"id":13001,"t":"Why Imaging Matters","s":"Which statement correctly distinguishes a radiologist from a radiographer?","o":["Radiologist: a doctor who interprets; radiographer: an AHP who produces images","Radiologist: an AHP who produces images; radiographer: a doctor who interprets them","Radiologist: a doctor who produces images; radiographer: an AHP who never reports","Radiologist and radiographer: both doctors, with different sub-specialties","Radiologist: an AHP who interprets; radiographer: a doctor who does biopsies"],"e":"<b>Radiologist</b>: a <b>doctor</b> (consultant) who <b>interprets</b> images and may run clinics, do biopsies and interventional radiology; many sub-specialise (orthopaedic, neuro, cardiac, paediatric). <b>Radiographer</b>: an <b>AHP</b> who <b>produces</b> high-quality images safely and accurately, and often <b>reports</b> too. Memory aid: the radiogra<b>ph</b>er takes the <b>ph</b>otograph.","w":["Swapped.","Radiographers produce images and often report.","Radiographers are AHPs.","Radiologists are doctors."],"k":"Radiologist interprets; radiographer produces (and often reports).","rn":[["","What is a radiologist?","What is a radiologist?","A consultant (doctor); Interprets medical images; Across many modalities"],["","What 3 things does a radiologist do?","What 3 things does a radiologist do?","Interprets images; Clinics, biopsies, IR; Many specialise"],["","Radiologist vs radiographer: who are they?","Radiologist vs radiographer: who are they?","Radiologist: consultant doctor; Radiographer: allied healthcare professional"]]},{"id":13002,"t":"Why Imaging Matters","s":"Why must every doctor understand imaging?","o":["Most hospital patients need imaging, and it increasingly treats disease","Most hospital patients need imaging, but only radiologists use the results","Imaging is mainly used in surgery, so all surgeons need it","Imaging is being replaced by blood tests in most specialties","Imaging is needed mainly for anatomy teaching in medical school"],"e":"Most patients attending hospital need referral to radiology; anatomy is demonstrated through imaging; imaging has expanded massively over 20 years, is <b>central to diagnosis</b>, and has an <b>expanding role in treatment</b> (interventional radiology).","w":["All doctors use it.","Not only surgery.","Imaging has expanded.","Central to diagnosis."],"k":"Radiology is central to diagnosis and treatment.","rn":[["","Why do most patients attending hospital need to go to the X-ray (radiology) department?","Why do most patients attending hospital need to go to the X-ray (radiology) department?","To aid diagnosis; To aid clinical management"],["","What does nuclear medicine do?","What does nuclear medicine do?","Assess organ function; Diagnose disease; Treat disease"],["","How are PACS images viewed in hospital?","How are PACS images viewed in hospital?","On monitors around the hospital"]]},{"id":13003,"t":"Modalities and Radiation","s":"Which timeline of imaging milestones is correct?","o":["1895 X-rays → 1896 fluoroscopy → 1971 CT → 1977 MRI","1895 X-rays → 1896 CT → 1971 fluoroscopy → 1977 MRI","1895 fluoroscopy → 1896 X-rays → 1971 CT → 1977 MRI","1895 X-rays → 1896 fluoroscopy → 1971 MRI → 1977 CT","1896 X-rays → 1913 fluoroscopy → 1957 CT → 1977 MRI"],"e":"Milestones: <b>1895</b> X-rays (Röntgen); <b>1896</b> fluoroscopy (Edison) and first UK radiology department; 1913 X-ray tube; 1950 PET; early 1950s nuclear medicine; 1955 image intensifier (C-arm); 1957 foetal ultrasound; <b>1971</b> CT (Hounsfield); <b>1977</b> first MRI scanner.","w":["CT was 1971.","X-rays first.","CT 1971, MRI 1977.","Dates wrong."],"k":"1895 X-ray, 1971 CT, 1977 MRI.","rn":[["","Which 3 imaging modalities use X-rays?","Which 3 imaging modalities use X-rays?","Radiography; Fluoroscopy; CT"]]},{"id":13004,"t":"History of Imaging","s":"Which statement about the earliest X-ray images and the people who made them is correct?","o":["His wife’s hand with her ring; early workers had radiation injury","His wife’s hand with her ring; early workers had no harmful effects","His own chest; early workers had radiation injury","Edison’s skull; early workers had electrical burns","His wife’s hand with her ring; early workers were well protected"],"e":"The first famous image was an <b>X-ray of Röntgen's wife's hand</b> (her ring visible). Early workers <b>suffered the effects of radiation exposure</b> because the dangers weren't understood and there was no protection.","w":["They suffered.","The hand.","Röntgen’s wife’s hand.","No protection."],"k":"First X-ray: wife's hand; early radiation harm."},{"id":13005,"t":"Fluoroscopy","s":"What is the C-arm, and when was the image intensifier in use?","o":["A mobile fluoroscopy unit used in theatre, from 1955","A mobile fluoroscopy unit used in theatre, from 1896","A fixed CT scanner used in A&E, from 1971","A mobile ultrasound unit used in theatre, from 1957","A gamma camera used in nuclear medicine, from 1950"],"e":"The <b>image intensifier</b> (“<b>C-arm</b>”) was in use by <b>1955</b>: the mobile fluoroscopy machine used in <b>theatre</b> (e.g. orthopaedic surgery) for live X-ray pictures.","w":["1955.","Fluoroscopy.","Not ultrasound.","Not a camera."],"k":"C-arm = mobile fluoroscopy, 1955.","rn":[["","What are 3 other uses of fluoroscopy?","What are 3 other uses of fluoroscopy?","Ba (barium) examinations; C arm in theatre; Urology procedures"]]},{"id":13006,"t":"Modalities and Radiation","s":"Which modalities do NOT use ionising radiation?","o":["Ultrasound and MRI","CT and fluoroscopy","Nuclear medicine and PET","Plain X-ray and CT","Fluoroscopy and ultrasound"],"e":"Only <b>ultrasound</b> (sound waves) and <b>MRI</b> (radio waves and a magnet) are free of ionising radiation. X-ray, fluoroscopy, CT, nuclear medicine and PET use it, so <b>benefit must outweigh risk</b>. Memory aid: “U and Me are safe”.","w":["Both ionising.","Both ionising.","Both ionising.","Fluoroscopy is ionising."],"k":"Non-ionising: ultrasound and MRI.","rn":[["","Which 2 modalities use no ionising radiation?","Which 2 modalities use no ionising radiation?","Ultrasound; MRI"],["","Non-ionising Radiation","Non-ionising Radiation","Safer forms of radiation; Sound waves (ultrasound); Radio waves (MRI)"],["","How do you remember the non-ionising modalities?","How do you remember the non-ionising modalities?","U and Me are safe; MNEMONIC"]]},{"id":13007,"t":"Modalities and Radiation","s":"What distinguishes ionising from non-ionising radiation?","o":["Ionising removes electrons and can damage DNA (X-rays, gamma)","Ionising adds electrons and can damage DNA (radio waves, sound)","Non-ionising removes electrons and can damage DNA (ultrasound)","Ionising removes electrons but cannot damage DNA (X-rays)","Ionising and non-ionising differ only in wavelength, not effect"],"e":"<b>Ionising</b> radiation <b>removes electrons from atoms</b> (can damage DNA): <b>X-rays, gamma rays</b> and some UV. <b>Non-ionising</b>: <b>sound waves</b> (ultrasound), <b>radio waves</b> (MRI).","w":["Removes; not radio/sound.","Non-ionising does not.","Can damage DNA.","They differ in effect."],"k":"Ionising removes electrons.","rn":[["","Ionising radiation","Ionising radiation","Removes electrons from atoms"],["","What are examples of ionising radiation?","What are examples of ionising radiation?","X-rays; Gamma rays; Some UV"]]},{"id":13008,"t":"Radiography","s":"Which list gives the strengths and weaknesses of plain radiography?","o":["Strengths: bone detail, cheap, available; weaknesses: 2D, ionising, poor soft tissue","Strengths: soft tissue, cheap, available; weaknesses: 2D, ionising, poor bone detail","Strengths: bone detail, 3D, cheap; weaknesses: ionising, operator-dependent","Strengths: bone detail, non-ionising; weaknesses: 2D, expensive","Strengths: functional detail, cheap; weaknesses: little anatomy"],"e":"Plain X-ray: <b>great spatial resolution</b> (bone), <b>cheap</b>, <b>readily available</b>; but <b>2D only</b> (what's behind the heart?), <b>ionising</b>, and <b>very limited soft-tissue</b> visualisation.","w":["Swapped.","2D only.","Ionising, cheap.","Nuclear medicine."],"k":"X-ray strengths and weaknesses.","rn":[["","What are the 3 weaknesses of radiography?","What are the 3 weaknesses of radiography?","2D only; Ionising radiation; Very limited soft tissue visualisation"],["","What are the 3 strengths of radiography?","What are the 3 strengths of radiography?","Great spatial resolution, especially bone; Readily available; Cheap"],["","What are the 2 weaknesses of CT?","What are the 2 weaknesses of CT?","Ionising radiation; Limited soft tissue contrast"]]},{"id":13009,"t":"Radiography","s":"A gas bubble with an air-fluid level is seen behind the heart shadow on a chest X-ray. Which weakness of plain radiography makes this easy to miss, and what was the example?","o":["It is 2D, so structures overlap – a hiatus hernia behind the heart","It is 2D, so structures overlap – a lung lobe collapse behind the heart","It is ionising, so the stomach is poorly shown – a hiatus hernia","It is operator-dependent – a hiatus hernia behind the heart","It cannot show gas – a hiatus hernia behind the heart"],"e":"An X-ray is a <b>2D shadow</b>, so structures overlap (“what is behind the heart?”). Her example: a <b>hiatus hernia</b>, where part of the stomach sits behind the heart, showing a gas bubble with an <b>air-fluid level</b>.","w":["Hiatus hernia.","2D overlap.","Not operator-dependent.","It shows gas."],"k":"2D overlap hides structures (hiatus hernia).","rn":[["","On X-ray, why ask \"what is behind the heart\"?","On X-ray, why ask \"what is behind the heart\"?","X-ray is 2D only; Structures overlap the heart"],["","Hiatus hernia","Hiatus hernia","Stomach up through diaphragm; Sits behind the heart"]]},{"id":13010,"t":"Radiography","f":"im14","s":"How are X-rays produced in an X-ray tube?","o":["Electrons from a heated cathode hit a rotating tungsten target","Electrons from a heated anode hit a rotating tungsten cathode","Protons from a heated cathode hit a rotating tungsten target","Electrons from a heated cathode hit a fixed copper target","A radioactive source in the tube emits X-rays through a window"],"e":"Electrons are fired from a heated <b>cathode</b> across a high voltage onto a (rotating) <b>tungsten target</b> (anode); X-rays are given off and leave through a window.","w":["Cathode → anode.","Electrons.","Tungsten, rotating.","Not radioactive."],"k":"Cathode → tungsten anode → X-rays.","rn":[["","Where do the electrons go in an X-ray tube?","Where do the electrons go in an X-ray tube?","Across to the rotating anode; Hit the tungsten target"]]},{"id":13011,"t":"The Chest X-ray","s":"Why does the radiographer use the light beam to collimate?","o":["To restrict the field, reducing dose and improving the image","To restrict the field, increasing dose but improving the image","To widen the field, so more of the body is imaged","To heat the cathode before the exposure","To magnify the heart on the image"],"e":"<b>Collimation</b> restricts the X-ray field to just the area needed, <b>reducing radiation dose</b> and improving the image.","w":["Reduces dose.","Restrict.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Collimation reduces dose.","rn":[["","What are the 3 benefits of a standing PA CXR?","What are the 3 benefits of a standing PA CXR?","Avoids magnification of heart; Allows good inspiration; Reduces dose to others"]]},{"id":13012,"t":"Radiography","s":"Why does bone appear white and air black on an X-ray?","o":["Bone absorbs photons; air transmits them","Bone transmits photons; air absorbs them","Bone scatters photons; air absorbs them","Bone and air both scatter photons","Bone emits photons; air reflects them"],"e":"Photons may be <b>absorbed</b> (don't reach the receptor → <b>white</b>, e.g. bone), <b>transmitted</b> (pass straight through → <b>black</b>, e.g. air) or <b>scattered</b> (deflected; adds fog and staff dose). Memory aid A-T-S.","w":["Swapped.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Absorbed = white; transmitted = black.","rn":[["","What are the 3 things that can happen to X-ray photons?","What are the 3 things that can happen to X-ray photons?","Scattered; Absorbed; Transmitted; MNEMONIC"],["","Absorbed photons","Absorbed photons","Lose energy; Don't reach image receptor"]]},{"id":13013,"t":"Radiography","s":"What is the purpose of filtration of the X-ray beam before it reaches the patient?","o":["To remove low-energy photons that add dose but not image","To remove high-energy photons that add dose but not image","To add low-energy photons that improve the image","To increase scatter for better contrast","To restrict the field to the area needed"],"e":"<b>Most</b> low-energy photons are <b>absorbed by filtration</b> before reaching the patient, removing useless photons that would only add dose.","w":["Low-energy.","Removes.","Scatter adds fog.","That is collimation."],"k":"Filtration removes low-energy photons.","rn":[["","Why are most X-ray photons absorbed by filtration?","Why are most X-ray photons absorbed by filtration?","They are low energy"]]},{"id":13014,"t":"Radiography","f":"im16","s":"List the four tissue densities on a plain X-ray from black to white.","o":["Air, fat, soft tissue/fluid, bone","Air, soft tissue/fluid, fat, bone","Fat, air, soft tissue/fluid, bone","Bone, soft tissue/fluid, fat, air","Air, fat, bone, soft tissue/fluid"],"e":"Four densities, black → white: <b>air</b> (lungs, trachea, stomach bubble), <b>fat</b> (dark grey), <b>soft tissue/fluid</b> (heart, diaphragm, vessels, muscle), <b>bone</b> (white). Metal and contrast are whiter still.","w":["Fat before soft tissue.","Air is blackest.","Reversed.","Bone whitest."],"k":"Air, fat, soft tissue, bone.","rn":[["","What are the 4 tissue densities on a CXR?","What are the 4 tissue densities on a CXR?","Air; Fat; Soft tissue/fluid; Bone"]]},{"id":13015,"t":"Radiography","s":"On a correctly marked chest X-ray, where does the patient's left side appear, and why?","o":["On the viewer’s right, as if facing the patient","On the viewer’s left, as if standing behind the patient","On the viewer’s right, as if standing behind the patient","On the viewer’s left, as if facing the patient","It varies, so there is no convention"],"e":"A CXR is viewed <b>as if facing the patient</b>, like a mirror: the <b>patient's left is on the viewer's right</b>. The heart (pointing to the patient's left) appears on the right of the screen. Always check the <b>L/R marker</b>.","w":["Facing.","Facing.","Right.","There is."],"k":"Patient's left = viewer's right."},{"id":13016,"t":"The Chest X-ray","s":"How many lobes does each lung have, and why the difference?","o":["Right 3, left 2 – the heart takes space on the left","Right 2, left 3 – the heart takes space on the right","Right 3, left 2 – the liver takes space on the left","Right 3, left 3 – both lungs are the same","Right 2, left 2 – both lungs are the same"],"e":"<b>Right has 3</b> lobes (upper, middle, lower), <b>left has 2</b> (upper, lower) – “the heart takes the space”.","w":["Swapped.","The heart.","Different.","3 and 2."],"k":"Right 3 lobes, left 2.","rn":[["","Axial abdominal CT: which liver lobes are seen?","Axial abdominal CT: which liver lobes are seen?","Left and right"]]},{"id":13017,"t":"Radiography","f":"im17","s":"In a child's elbow X-ray, a raised (displaced) fat pad is seen but no fracture line. What does this suggest?","o":["An effusion with an occult fracture (e.g. supracondylar)","An effusion with no fracture, from normal ossification","Normal ossification centres, as expected in children","An occult fracture of the radius without an effusion","A lipohaemarthrosis of the elbow joint"],"e":"In children the elbow has many normal <b>ossification centres</b> that can look like fragments; a <b>displaced (raised) fat pad</b> suggests an <b>effusion</b> and a hidden fracture, e.g. a <b>supracondylar fracture</b>.","w":["Suggests occult fracture.","Fat pad is the clue.","Effusion.","Knee example."],"k":"Raised elbow fat pad = occult fracture."},{"id":13018,"t":"Radiography","s":"A cross-table lateral knee X-ray after trauma shows a fat-fluid level in the joint. Name this finding.","e":"Lipohaemarthrosis: fat (from marrow) and blood in the joint, meaning an intra-articular fracture until proven otherwise.","k":"Lipohaemarthrosis.","a":["lipohaemarthrosis","lipohemarthrosis","lipo-haemarthrosis"],"rn":[["","Lipohaemarthrosis","Lipohaemarthrosis","Fat-fluid level; In the joint"]]},{"id":13019,"t":"Fluoroscopy","s":"What is fluoroscopy, and which uses are listed?","o":["Real-time X-rays: barium, angiography, stents, C-arm, urology","Real-time continuous X-rays: barium studies, bone scans, PET and urology","Real-time sound pulses: barium, angiography, stents and urology","Static X-ray images: barium, angiography, stents and urology","Cross-sectional magnet imaging: angiography and stents"],"e":"<b>Fluoroscopy</b> is a <b>real-time</b> examination using <b>continuous production of X-rays</b>: <b>barium</b> examinations, <b>angiography</b>, <b>cardiac</b> procedures (stents), the <b>C-arm in theatre</b>, <b>urology</b>.","w":["Not bone scans or PET.","X-rays.","Real time.","Not MRI."],"k":"Fluoroscopy = live X-ray.","rn":[["","What are 3 other uses of fluoroscopy?","What are 3 other uses of fluoroscopy?","Ba (barium) examinations; C arm in theatre; Urology procedures"],["","Fluoroscopy","Fluoroscopy","Real time moving structures; Continuous X-rays; A series of images"]]},{"id":13020,"t":"Fluoroscopy","f":"im20","s":"A barium enema shows an “apple-core” narrowing of the bowel. What is the classic diagnosis?","o":["Colorectal carcinoma","Hiatus hernia","Lipohaemarthrosis","Bone metastases","Fallopian tube patency"],"e":"An “<b>apple-core</b>” narrowing on barium enema is the classic appearance of a <b>colorectal carcinoma</b>.","w":["CXR finding.","Knee.","Bone scan.","HSG."],"k":"Apple-core = colorectal carcinoma.","rn":[["","Apple core carcinoma","Apple core carcinoma","Tumour compresses the bowel; Looks like an apple core"],["","Why does an apple core carcinoma matter?","Why does an apple core carcinoma matter?","Limits what gets through"]]},{"id":13021,"t":"Fluoroscopy","s":"During a hysterosalpingogram, contrast spills from the ends of the fallopian tubes. What does this show?","o":["The tubes are patent","The tubes are blocked","The uterus is perforated","The ovaries are enlarged","The contrast is in the bladder"],"e":"HSG: contrast injected into the uterus; if it <b>spills out of the ends of the fallopian tubes</b> they are <b>patent</b>. Used in fertility investigations.","w":["Spill = patent.","Not stated.","Not stated.","Not stated."],"k":"HSG spill = patent tubes.","rn":[["","What is seen on an HSG?","What is seen on an HSG?","Uterus; Right and left tubes; Spill of dye"]]},{"id":13022,"t":"Computed Tomography","s":"Which strengths and weaknesses describe CT?","o":["Cross-sectional, good resolution, available; ionising, weaker soft tissue than MRI","Cross-sectional, good resolution, available; non-ionising, best soft tissue","Cross-sectional, operator-dependent; ionising, weaker soft tissue than MRI","2D, good resolution, cheap; ionising, weaker soft tissue than MRI","Cross-sectional, functional; ionising, little anatomy"],"e":"CT: <b>cross-sectional</b> (no overlap), good <b>spatial resolution</b>, <b>widely available</b>; but <b>ionising</b> (relatively high dose) and <b>limited soft-tissue contrast</b> compared with MRI.","w":["Ionising.","Not operator-dependent.","Cross-sectional.","Nuclear medicine."],"k":"CT strengths and weaknesses.","rn":[["","What are the 3 strengths of CT?","What are the 3 strengths of CT?","Cross-sectional; Spatial resolution; Widely available"]]},{"id":13023,"t":"Computed Tomography","s":"Who developed CT, and what anecdote is told about its funding?","o":["Godfrey Hounsfield at EMI, reportedly from Beatles profits","Godfrey Hounsfield at EMI, funded by the NHS","Wilhelm Röntgen at EMI, reportedly from Beatles profits","Thomas Edison at EMI, reportedly from Beatles profits","Godfrey Hounsfield at GE, reportedly from Beatles profits"],"e":"CT was developed in the early 1970s by <b>Godfrey Hounsfield</b> at <b>EMI</b>, reportedly from the <b>profits of Beatles records</b> (the lecturer said she didn't know if true). His name lives on in the <b>Hounsfield unit</b>.","w":["Beatles.","Hounsfield.","Hounsfield.","EMI."],"k":"Hounsfield, EMI, Beatles anecdote."},{"id":13024,"t":"Computed Tomography","s":"Why can a modern CT scan be completed in a single breath hold?","o":["Helical acquisition: the patient moves through a rotating beam","Helical acquisition: the beam moves along a stationary patient","Single-slice acquisition: one image per breath","A stronger magnet speeds up proton recovery","A wider ultrasound probe covers more area"],"e":"Modern scanners mostly use <b>helical (spiral)</b> acquisition: the <b>patient moves through a rotating X-ray beam</b> and detectors, creating <b>3D data</b> reconstructable in any plane, with very short scan times – a whole scan in a <b>single breath hold</b>. Once it took 5 minutes per slice.","w":["Patient moves.","Many slices.","Not MRI.","Not ultrasound."],"k":"Helical CT → single breath hold.","rn":[["","How do modern CT scanners acquire images?","How do modern CT scanners acquire images?","Helical or spiral; Patient moves through rotating beam; Creates 3D data"]]},{"id":13025,"t":"Computed Tomography","f":"im24","s":"A CT scan with intravenous contrast shows bright kidneys and vessels; a coronal knee CT is used for planning surgery. Which statements are correct?","o":["Contrast brightens vessels and kidneys; CT helps plan fracture surgery","Contrast darkens vessels and kidneys; CT helps plan fracture surgery","Contrast brightens vessels and kidneys; CT cannot show bone detail","Contrast brightens bone only; CT helps plan fracture surgery","Contrast is used only in MRI; CT helps plan fracture surgery"],"e":"CT with <b>IV contrast</b> makes vessels and organs such as the <b>kidneys look bright</b>. A <b>coronal CT of the knee</b> was shown for <b>surgical planning</b> of complex fractures (CT reconstructs any plane).","w":["Bright.","It can.","Vessels/organs.","CT too."],"k":"CT contrast and reconstruction uses.","img":{"src":"13/ctcontrast","cap":"Left: axial CT abdomen; right: coronal CT knee."},"rn":[["","Why give contrast on CT?","Why give contrast on CT?","Helps differentiate the different organs"],["","How can you tell the CT patient had injected contrast?","How can you tell the CT patient had injected contrast?","Contrast in the kidneys"],["","What are contrast agents used for?","What are contrast agents used for?","Better visualisation of; Organs, blood vessels; Pathology"]]},{"id":13026,"t":"Ultrasound","s":"How does ultrasound create an image?","o":["A transducer sends sound pulses; their reflections form the image","A transducer sends X-ray pulses; their reflections form the image","A transducer receives sound from the body without sending any","A transducer sends radio pulses that flip protons","A gamma camera detects sound from a tracer"],"e":"Ultrasound is a <b>pressure (sound) wave</b>; pulses are transmitted by a <b>handheld transducer</b>, and <b>reflection</b> back to the transducer provides the image. Developed for therapy in the 1930s, diagnosis from the 1950s.","w":["Sound.","It sends pulses.","MRI.","Not."],"k":"Ultrasound = reflected sound pulses.","rn":[["","What provides the ultrasound image?","What provides the ultrasound image?","Reflected sound; Back to the transducer"]]},{"id":13027,"t":"Ultrasound","s":"Which strengths and weaknesses describe ultrasound?","o":["Non-ionising, dynamic, Doppler; operator-dependent, blocked by bone/gas","Non-ionising, dynamic, Doppler; not operator-dependent, sees through gas","Ionising, dynamic, Doppler; operator-dependent, blocked by bone/gas","Non-ionising, static, 2D only; operator-dependent","Non-ionising, superb bone detail; blocked by soft tissue"],"e":"Ultrasound: <b>non-ionising</b>, <b>multiplanar</b>, <b>dynamic</b> (real time), <b>Doppler</b> (blood flow); but <b>operator-dependent</b> and <b>cannot see through bone or gas</b>.","w":["Operator-dependent; blocked by gas.","Non-ionising.","Multiplanar, dynamic.","Bone blocks it."],"k":"Ultrasound strengths/weaknesses.","rn":[["","What are the 2 weaknesses of ultrasound?","What are the 2 weaknesses of ultrasound?","Operator-dependent; Can't see bone or gas"],["","What are the 4 strengths of ultrasound?","What are the 4 strengths of ultrasound?","Non-ionising radiation; Multiplanar; Dynamic; Doppler"]]},{"id":13028,"t":"Ultrasound","s":"Why is ultrasound described as operator-dependent?","o":["The operator chooses views, and often only snapshots are saved","The operator chooses views, and the whole scan is always saved","The machine chooses views automatically, so results vary","It needs no training, so quality varies between users","It produces identical images regardless of the operator"],"e":"Ultrasound is very <b>operator-dependent</b>: the operator chooses what to look at, and often only <b>single snapshot images</b> are saved, so it is hard for someone else to review later.","w":["Snapshots.","Operator chooses.","Training matters.","Not identical."],"k":"Operator-dependent: limited saved images.","rn":[["","Why is ultrasound operator-dependent?","Why is ultrasound operator-dependent?","Depends on operator skill; Often only snapshots kept"]]},{"id":13029,"t":"Ultrasound","s":"Name the ultrasound technique that shows blood flow in colour.","e":"Doppler ultrasound shows blood flow, often in colour.","k":"Doppler.","a":["doppler","colour doppler","color doppler","doppler ultrasound"],"rn":[["","Which ultrasound type shows blood flow?","Which ultrasound type shows blood flow?","Doppler"],["","Doppler","Doppler","Shows blood flow; Through vessels"]]},{"id":13030,"t":"MRI","s":"Which strengths and weaknesses describe MRI?","o":["Non-ionising, superb soft tissue; strong magnet, costly, misses calcium/gas","Non-ionising, superb bone detail; strong magnet, costly, misses calcium/gas","Ionising, superb soft tissue; strong magnet, costly, misses calcium/gas","Non-ionising, superb soft tissue; cheap and widely available","Non-ionising, superb soft tissue; operator-dependent, 2D only"],"e":"MRI: <b>non-ionising</b>, <b>multiplanar</b>, <b>exquisite soft-tissue contrast</b>; but limited availability to some referrers, <b>VERY strong magnetic field</b>, some patients/equipment unsuitable, <b>expensive shielding</b>, <b>cannot see calcification or gas</b>.","w":["Soft tissue.","Non-ionising.","Expensive, limited.","Multiplanar."],"k":"MRI strengths/weaknesses.","rn":[["","What are the 3 strengths of MRI?","What are the 3 strengths of MRI?","Non-ionising; Multiplanar; Exquisite soft tissue contrast"]]},{"id":13031,"t":"MRI","s":"A patient has a suspected prolapsed intervertebral disc. Which modality is most appropriate guidance?","o":["MRI, because it is best for soft tissue","CT, because it is best for soft tissue","Plain X-ray, because it shows soft tissue well","Nuclear medicine bone scan","Ultrasound through the spine"],"e":"Use <b>MRI for soft tissue</b> (e.g. a <b>prolapsed disc</b>) and <b>CT for bone</b>.","w":["CT is for bone.","Poor soft tissue.","Function, metastases.","Can't see through bone."],"k":"Soft tissue → MRI; bone → CT.","rn":[["","What does MRI show best?","What does MRI show best?","Soft tissue; Fine structures in great detail"],["","What are the 3 strengths of MRI?","What are the 3 strengths of MRI?","Non-ionising; Multiplanar; Exquisite soft tissue contrast"],["","What are the 4 tissue densities on a CXR?","What are the 4 tissue densities on a CXR?","Air; Fat; Soft tissue/fluid; Bone"]]},{"id":13032,"t":"MRI","s":"Which statement about the MRI scanner is correct?","o":["1.5–3 T; niobium-titanium cooled by liquid helium at −269 °C","1.5–3 T; niobium-titanium cooled by liquid nitrogen at −196 °C","15–30 T; niobium-titanium cooled by liquid helium at −269 °C","1.5–3 T; tungsten anode cooled by liquid helium","1.5–3 T; copper coil cooled by water"],"e":"MRI: <b>1.5–3 Tesla</b>; magnet is a <b>niobium-titanium superconductor</b> cooled by <b>liquid helium (−269 °C)</b>; an <b>RF coil</b> emits radio-frequency pulses. New development: almost helium-free MRI, due to helium scarcity.","w":["Helium.","1.5–3 T.","Tungsten is X-ray.","Superconductor, helium."],"k":"MRI: 1.5–3 T, helium-cooled superconductor.","rn":[["","What cools the MRI magnet?","What cools the MRI magnet?","Liquid helium; -269°C"]]},{"id":13033,"t":"MRI","s":"Put the basic MRI process in order.","o":["Protons align → RF flips them → tissue-dependent recovery → contrast","RF flips protons → protons align → tissue-dependent recovery → contrast","Protons align → RF flips them → identical recovery → no contrast","Electrons align → RF flips them → recovery → contrast","Protons align → X-rays flip them → recovery → contrast"],"e":"MRI basics: patient in scanner → <b>hydrogen protons align</b> with the magnet → <b>RF pulse</b> flips them → <b>recovery rate depends on the tissue</b> → image has a range of contrast between tissues.","w":["Align first.","Recovery depends on tissue.","Hydrogen protons.","RF pulse."],"k":"MRI: align → flip → tissue-dependent recovery.","rn":[["","MRI: what gives image contrast?","MRI: what gives image contrast?","Recovery rate depends on tissue; Range of contrast"],["","MRI: what does the RF pulse do?","MRI: what does the RF pulse do?","Flips the protons"]]},{"id":13034,"t":"MRI","s":"These three images are the same brain level. Which labelling of the sequences is correct?","o":["A T1; B FLAIR; C T2","A T2; B FLAIR; C T1","A T1; B T2; C FLAIR","A FLAIR; B T1; C T2","A T2; B T1; C FLAIR"],"e":"T1: CSF dark, good for anatomy. T2: CSF bright, good for pathology (oedema, fluid). FLAIR: fluid suppressed, so lesions beside CSF stand out.","w":["C has bright CSF in the ventricles: T2. A has dark CSF with crisp anatomy: T1.","B has dark CSF but T2-like grey/white contrast: FLAIR.","A has dark CSF and grey-white contrast suited to anatomy: T1.","A is T1, B is FLAIR."],"k":"Bright CSF = T2; dark CSF + T2 look = FLAIR.","img":{"src":"13/mriseq","cap":"Axial brain MRI, same level, three sequences. Labels removed."},"rn":[["","What are the 3 brain MRI sequences compared?","What are the 3 brain MRI sequences compared?","T1; FLAIR; T2"]]},{"id":13035,"t":"MRI","s":"Name the sequence shown in <b>C</b>, in which CSF in the ventricles is bright.","e":"C is T2: fluid (CSF, oedema) is bright, so it is good for pathology.","k":"C = T2.","img":{"src":"13/mriseq","cap":"Axial brain MRI, same level, three sequences. Labels removed."},"a":["t2","t2 weighted","t2-weighted","t2w"],"rn":[["","T2 vs T1: CSF","T2 vs T1: CSF","T2: CSF bright; T1: CSF dark"]]},{"id":13036,"t":"MRI","s":"Why must staff remember that “the magnet is ALWAYS on”?","o":["It is never switched off, so metal can be pulled in any time","It is switched off between scans, but metal remains magnetic","It is on only during scans, so screening happens then","It is a temporary magnet that recharges overnight","It is on only while the RF coil is active"],"e":"<b>The magnet is ALWAYS on</b>: it cannot simply be switched off; metal objects can be pulled in with huge force (a hospital bed stuck to the magnet). Switching off requires a costly emergency <b>quench</b>.","w":["Always on.","Always on.","Superconducting.","Always on."],"k":"MRI magnet always on."},{"id":13037,"t":"PACS and Which Modality","s":"Which of the following is <b>not</b> a contraindication to MRI?","o":["A hip replacement","Metal fragments in the eye","Aneurysm clips","A cochlear implant","An implanted defibrillator"],"e":"Contraindications include some pacemakers, ICDs, aneurysm clips, neurostimulators, insulin/infusion pumps, drug infusion devices, bone growth stimulators, cochlear implants, metal in the eye. <b>Orthopaedic hardware is NOT</b> a contraindication (may cause artefact).","w":["Contraindication.","Contraindication.","Contraindication.","Contraindication."],"k":"Orthopaedic hardware is OK for MRI.","rn":[["","What did PACS replace?","What did PACS replace?","Hard copy X-ray films"]]},{"id":13038,"t":"MRI","s":"A metal worker is booked for an MRI. Why might they need screening first, sometimes with an orbit X-ray?","o":["Metal fragments in the eye can move and damage it","Metal fragments in the eye block the RF signal","Metal workers often have pacemakers fitted","To measure bone density before scanning","To check for contrast allergy"],"e":"<b>Metal fragments in the eye</b> (e.g. metal workers) can <b>move</b> in the field and damage the eye, so patients are screened (sometimes with an orbit X-ray).","w":["Movement.","Not stated.","Not stated.","Not stated."],"k":"Eye metal screening before MRI.","rn":[["","Why are metal fragments in the eye dangerous in MRI?","Why are metal fragments in the eye dangerous in MRI?","Fragments will move; Risk of compromising sight"]]},{"id":13039,"t":"MRI","s":"In an emergency, the MRI magnet is switched off by rapidly boiling off its helium. Name this.","e":"A quench rapidly boils off the helium to switch the magnet off; it is very costly.","k":"Quench.","a":["quench","quenching","a quench"],"rn":[["","Quench","Quench","Releases the helium; Turns off the magnet"]]},{"id":13040,"t":"MRI","s":"How long does an MRI scan take compared with CT, and what option exists for claustrophobic patients?","o":["10–15 up to 45 minutes; open scanners exist","10–15 up to 45 seconds; open scanners exist","10–15 up to 45 minutes; no options exist","Several hours; sedation is always needed","One breath hold, like CT"],"e":"MRI takes about <b>10–15 minutes up to 45 minutes</b>, much longer than CT. <b>Open MRI scanners</b> exist for patients with <b>claustrophobia</b>.","w":["Minutes.","Open MRI.","Not stated.","Longer than CT."],"k":"MRI is slower; open scanners exist.","rn":[["","Which scanners help patients with claustrophobia?","Which scanners help patients with claustrophobia?","Open MRI scanners"]]},{"id":13041,"t":"Nuclear Medicine and PET","s":"Which statement describes nuclear medicine?","o":["IV tracers detected by a gamma camera, assessing organ function","IV tracers detected by an X-ray receptor, showing detailed anatomy","Oral tracers detected by an MRI coil, showing organ function","Reflected sound pulses detected by a transducer","Continuous X-rays detected as a live video"],"e":"<b>Nuclear medicine</b>: small amounts of radioactive substances (tracers, usually IV) detected by a <b>gamma camera</b>, to assess <b>organ function</b>, diagnose and treat. Strength: functional information. Weakness: very limited anatomy, radiation to patient, staff and population.","w":["Function.","Gamma camera.","Ultrasound.","Fluoroscopy."],"k":"NM: tracers + gamma camera; function.","rn":[["","What detects the radiation in nuclear medicine?","What detects the radiation in nuclear medicine?","Gamma camera"]]},{"id":13042,"t":"Nuclear Medicine and PET","f":"im35","s":"A man with prostate cancer has a bone scan. What is it used to detect?","o":["Early spread of prostate cancer to bone","Early spread of prostate cancer to lymph nodes","Fractures of the pelvis and spine only","Soft-tissue tumours of the prostate itself","Blood flow in the prostate"],"e":"A <b>bone scan</b> is used to detect <b>early spread (metastases) of prostate cancer</b> to bone (anterior and posterior views).","w":["Bone.","Metastases.","Not stated.","Doppler."],"k":"Bone scan → prostate metastases.","rn":[["","Why is a bone scan good for prostate cancer?","Why is a bone scan good for prostate cancer?","Shows bone spread early; X-rays may show it later"]]},{"id":13043,"t":"Nuclear Medicine and PET","s":"After a nuclear medicine injection, what advice is given to the patient?","o":["Avoid pregnant women and young children for a while","Avoid all close contact with anyone at all for a full month","Avoid MRI scans for life","Avoid food and drink for 24 hours","No advice is needed after the injection"],"e":"After injection the patient is <b>radioactive</b> for a while, so they are told to <b>avoid close contact with pregnant women and young children</b> for a set period.","w":["A set period, with pregnant women and children.","Not stated.","Not stated.","Advice is given."],"k":"Post-NM radiation precautions.","rn":[["","What must patients do after a nuclear medicine injection?","What must patients do after a nuclear medicine injection?","Avoid people for a while"]]},{"id":13044,"t":"Nuclear Medicine and PET","s":"Name the glucose-analogue tracer that builds up in glucose-hungry tumours on PET.","e":"FDG (a glucose analogue) accumulates in tumours; brain, heart and bladder are normally “hot”.","k":"FDG.","a":["fdg","fluorodeoxyglucose","18f-fdg","f-18 fdg","fluoro-deoxyglucose"],"rn":[["","What is the patient injected with for PET?","What is the patient injected with for PET?","Radioactive radiopharmaceutical similar to glucose; Fluorodeoxyglucose (FDG)"],["","What does FDG do in tumour cells?","What does FDG do in tumour cells?","Accumulates and concentrates; Detected and quantified"],["","What are FDG accumulations on PET called?","What are FDG accumulations on PET called?","Hot spots"]]},{"id":13045,"t":"Nuclear Medicine and PET","f":"im37","s":"On FDG PET, which normal organs commonly appear “hot”, and why?","o":["Brain, heart and bladder – glucose use and urinary excretion","Brain, heart and liver – glucose use and biliary excretion","Lungs and bones – air and calcium","Muscles only – after exercise","None – normal tissue is never hot"],"e":"The <b>brain, heart and bladder</b> normally look “hot” on FDG PET: the brain uses glucose; FDG is excreted in urine.","w":["Bladder.","Not stated.","Not stated.","Normal hot areas exist."],"k":"Normal PET hot spots."},{"id":13046,"t":"Modalities and Radiation","f":"im36","s":"Which statement about hybrid imaging is correct?","o":["Fuses modalities (e.g. PET-CT); CT/MRI done first","It fuses modalities (e.g. PET-CT) for anatomy only, with PET done first","It fuses modalities (e.g. US-CT) for function only; CT first","It uses one modality at two time points","It is widely available in every hospital"],"e":"<b>Hybrid imaging</b> fuses 2+ modalities (<b>PET-CT, PET-MRI</b>), giving <b>anatomy and function</b>; the <b>CT or MRI is done first</b>. It monitors disease progression (active tumours bright) but has <b>limited availability</b>. Example: PET-CT for staging probable lung cancer showed <b>adrenal metastases</b>.","w":["Anatomy and function; CT/MRI first.","Not stated.","Two modalities.","Limited."],"k":"Hybrid = anatomy + function.","rn":[["","What are the 2 examples of hybrid imaging?","What are the 2 examples of hybrid imaging?","PET-CT; PET-MRI"],["","Which 2 modalities use radioactive tracers?","Which 2 modalities use radioactive tracers?","Nuclear medicine; PET CT/MRI"],["","Why is PET combined with CT/MRI?","Why is PET combined with CT/MRI?","PET images are noisy; To see anatomy easily"]]},{"id":13047,"t":"PACS and Which Modality","s":"What is PACS?","o":["Picture archiving and communications system: digital images since mid-2000s","Picture archiving and communications system: hard-copy films on light boxes","Patient access and contrast safety system","Portable anatomy and CT scanner","Picture analysis and computed slicing"],"e":"<b>PACS</b> = picture archiving and communications system. Images used to be hard-copy films on light boxes; since the <b>mid-2000s in the UK</b> they are <b>digital</b>, viewed on monitors throughout the hospital.","w":["Digital.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"PACS = digital image system.","rn":[["","PACS","PACS","Picture archiving and communications system; Digital X-ray/radiology system"]]},{"id":13048,"t":"MRI","f":"im40","s":"A pelvis image is a cross-sectional slice with excellent soft-tissue and muscle detail where cortical bone looks black. Which modality is it?","o":["MRI","CT","Plain X-ray","Ultrasound","Nuclear medicine"],"e":"Clue: slice with <b>excellent soft-tissue detail</b> and <b>cortical bone black</b> = <b>MRI</b>. CT: bone very white, soft tissue fairly uniform grey. Plain X-ray: 2D overlapping, bone white.","w":["Bone white.","2D.","Fan-shaped grainy.","Blotchy uptake."],"k":"Spot MRI by soft tissue and black cortex.","rn":[["","What does MRI show best?","What does MRI show best?","Soft tissue; Fine structures in great detail"]]},{"id":13049,"t":"Ultrasound","s":"These fan-shaped, grainy grey images were obtained at the bedside without radiation. Name the modality.","e":"Ultrasound: a transducer sends sound pulses and the reflections form a fan-shaped, grainy image. Non-ionising and dynamic, but operator-dependent and blocked by bone and gas.","k":"Ultrasound.","img":{"src":"13/us","cap":"Three images of abdominal organs."},"a":["ultrasound","us","ultrasonography","sonography","ultrasound scan"],"rn":[["","What are the 2 weaknesses of ultrasound?","What are the 2 weaknesses of ultrasound?","Operator-dependent; Can't see bone or gas"],["","What are the 4 strengths of ultrasound?","What are the 4 strengths of ultrasound?","Non-ionising radiation; Multiplanar; Dynamic; Doppler"],["","Non-ionising Radiation","Non-ionising Radiation","Safer forms of radiation; Sound waves (ultrasound); Radio waves (MRI)"]]},{"id":13050,"t":"Nuclear Medicine and PET","s":"A man with prostate cancer has these whole-body images after an IV tracer. Name the modality.","e":"A nuclear medicine bone scan: an IV tracer is detected by a gamma camera; areas of uptake (black) show function, e.g. early spread of prostate cancer to bone. Little anatomical detail.","k":"Nuclear medicine bone scan.","img":{"src":"13/bonescan","cap":"Whole-body images, anterior and posterior."},"a":["nuclear medicine","bone scan","isotope bone scan","scintigraphy","bone scintigraphy","gamma camera"],"rn":[["","Nuclear medicine bone scan: what can it show?","Nuclear medicine bone scan: what can it show?","Bone metastases; From prostate cancer; Dark hot spots"],["","What detects the radiation in nuclear medicine?","What detects the radiation in nuclear medicine?","Gamma camera"],["","Which 2 modalities use radioactive tracers?","Which 2 modalities use radioactive tracers?","Nuclear medicine; PET CT/MRI"]]},{"id":13051,"t":"Contrast Agents","s":"Which statement about iodinated contrast media is correct?","o":["Non-ionic, water-soluble iodine (270–320 mg I/ml) for CT","Non-ionic, water-soluble radio-opaque iodine (270–320 mg I/ml) for MRI","Ionic, fat-soluble, radio-opaque iodine (2.7–3.2 mg I/ml) for CT","Non-ionic, water-soluble, radioactive iodine for nuclear medicine","Insoluble barium suspension for CT and angiography"],"e":"<b>Iodinated contrast</b>: pharmacological agents bound to iodine, <b>radio-opaque</b> (white), <b>non-ionic, water-soluble</b>, most <b>270–320 mg I/ml</b>; used in <b>CT</b> and <b>angiography/interventional</b> procedures. Barium: fluoroscopy/gut. Gadolinium: MRI.","w":["MRI uses gadolinium.","Not.","Not radioactive.","Barium is for fluoroscopy."],"k":"Iodinated contrast for CT/angiography.","rn":[["","What is iodinated contrast?","What is iodinated contrast?","Bound to iodine; Radio-opaque; Non-ionic water-soluble"]]},{"id":13052,"t":"Contrast Agents","s":"Before giving iodinated contrast, which three things should be checked?","o":["Allergy, pregnancy and renal function","Allergy, pregnancy and liver function","Allergy, renal function and eye metal","Pregnancy, blood glucose and heart rate","Height, weight and blood group"],"e":"Before iodinated contrast check: <b>allergy history</b> (allergic reactions), <b>pregnancy</b> (contrast crosses to the foetus; avoid if possible) and <b>renal function</b> (contrast-induced nephrotoxicity).","w":["Renal.","Eye metal is MRI.","Not stated.","Not stated."],"k":"Contrast checks: allergy, pregnancy, renal.","rn":[["","Contrast: which 2 kidney risks?","Contrast: which 2 kidney risks?","Contrast induced nephrotoxicity; Poor renal function"],["","What must be established before a contrast exam?","What must be established before a contrast exam?","Patient's renal function"]]},{"id":13053,"t":"Contrast Agents","s":"A patient with poor kidney function needs a contrast CT. What is the correct approach?","o":["Check renal function and decide on risk/benefit","Proceed without checking, as risk is low","Cancel all contrast scans in renal impairment","Double the dose to improve image quality","Use gadolinium instead without checks"],"e":"<b>Contrast-induced nephrotoxicity</b>: establish <b>renal function</b> (e.g. eGFR) first; with poor function the exam is done on a <b>risk/benefit</b> assessment. The lecturer called this the worst outcome to worry about.","w":["Check.","Risk/benefit.","Not.","NSF risk."],"k":"Renal function before contrast.","rn":[["","Poor renal function: is contrast still used?","Poor renal function: is contrast still used?","Risk/benefit assessment; Go ahead if benefit outweighs"],["","Contrast: which 2 kidney risks?","Contrast: which 2 kidney risks?","Contrast induced nephrotoxicity; Poor renal function"]]},{"id":13054,"t":"Contrast Agents","s":"A patient with renal failure develops firm skin patches with oedema weeks after a contrast MRI. Name the condition.","e":"Nephrogenic systemic fibrosis is linked to gadolinium in poor renal function; firm skin patches with oedema over weeks, sometimes affecting lungs and heart.","k":"NSF.","a":["nephrogenic systemic fibrosis","nsf"],"rn":[["","What does nephrogenic systemic fibrosis affect?","What does nephrogenic systemic fibrosis affect?","Mainly skin; Also lungs, heart, muscles"],["","Nephrogenic systemic fibrosis","Nephrogenic systemic fibrosis","Related to Gd (gadolinium) contrast"]]},{"id":13055,"t":"Contrast Agents","s":"Which pairing of contrast agent and modality is correct?","o":["Barium – fluoroscopy; gadolinium – MRI; iodine – CT","Barium – MRI; gadolinium – fluoroscopy; iodine – CT","Barium – fluoroscopy; gadolinium – CT; iodine – MRI","Barium – CT; gadolinium – MRI; iodine – ultrasound","FDG – CT; gadolinium – MRI; iodine – fluoroscopy"],"e":"<b>Barium</b>: fluoroscopy (gut); <b>gadolinium</b>: MRI; <b>iodinated</b>: CT and angiography.","w":["Swapped.","Swapped.","Not.","FDG is PET."],"k":"Contrast–modality pairs.","rn":[["","What are the 3 contrast examples?","What are the 3 contrast examples?","Barium swallow; MRI before/after gadolinium (meningioma); CT pre vs post contrast"]]},{"id":13056,"t":"Contrast Agents","s":"How may contrast be given and eliminated?","o":["Injected, swallowed or rectal; out via urine, rectum or absorbed","Injected only; eliminated via urine only","Swallowed only; never eliminated","Inhaled or injected; eliminated via the lungs and the skin","Topically; absorbed through the skin"],"e":"Contrast may be <b>injected, swallowed or given rectally</b>; eliminated via <b>urine</b>, <b>rectally</b>, or <b>absorbed</b>.","w":["More routes.","Eliminated.","Not stated.","Not."],"k":"Contrast routes.","rn":[["","What are the 3 ways contrast is eliminated?","What are the 3 ways contrast is eliminated?","Urine; Rectally; Absorbed"],["","What are the 3 ways contrast can be given?","What are the 3 ways contrast can be given?","Injected; Swallowed; Administered rectally"]]},{"id":13057,"t":"Image Orientation","f":"im46","s":"Which pairing of imaging plane and description is correct?","o":["Axial: slices viewed from the feet; sagittal: splits left from right","Axial: slices viewed from the head; sagittal: splits front from back","Axial: splits left from right; sagittal: slices viewed from the feet","Coronal: slices viewed from the feet; sagittal: splits left from right","Axial: slices viewed from the feet; coronal: splits left from right"],"e":"<b>Axial (transverse)</b>: slices across the body, viewed <b>from the feet up</b>. <b>Coronal (frontal)</b>: front/back halves. <b>Sagittal (lateral)</b>: down the middle, left/right. Memory aid: sagittal = arrow; coronal = crown.","w":["Feet; sagittal L/R.","Swapped.","Axial.","Coronal is front/back."],"k":"Axial, coronal, sagittal.","rn":[["","How do you remember the planes?","How do you remember the planes?","Sagittal = arrow; Coronal = crown; Axial = salami slices; MNEMONIC"]]},{"id":13058,"t":"Image Orientation","s":"A chest X-ray shows the heart on the right; the marker and image are correct. Name the genuine condition.","e":"Heart on the “wrong” side: either a wrong marker/flipped image or genuine dextrocardia.","k":"Dextrocardia.","a":["dextrocardia"],"rn":[["","Dextrocardia","Dextrocardia","Heart on the opposite side"],["","If the heart appears on the right on a CXR, what are the 2 possibilities?","If the heart appears on the right on a CXR, what are the 2 possibilities?","Incorrect marker/processed wrongly; Dextrocardia"]]},{"id":13059,"t":"The Chest X-ray","s":"What is the difference between an AP and a PA projection?","o":["AP: beam front to back; PA: beam back to front","AP: beam back to front; PA: beam front to back","AP and PA are the same projection","AP is used in CT; PA in plain films","AP: receptor in front; PA: receptor behind"],"e":"<b>AP</b> (anteroposterior): beam passes <b>front → back</b>. <b>PA</b> (posteroanterior): beam passes <b>back → front</b> (receptor in front). Memory aid: PA = Posterior first.","w":["Swapped.","Different.","Not.","Swapped."],"k":"AP vs PA beam direction.","rn":[["","How does the patient stand for a CXR?","How does the patient stand for a CXR?","Chest to the X-ray receptor; PA (back to front)"]]},{"id":13060,"t":"Interventional Radiology","s":"What is interventional radiology?","o":["Image-guided procedures via the skin or a body opening","Image-guided reporting of plain films, CT and MRI scans","Surgical procedures without imaging guidance","Building and maintaining scanners","Archiving images for later review"],"e":"<b>IR</b>: an <b>image-guided diagnostic or therapeutic procedure</b> through <b>percutaneous puncture</b> (through the skin) of a vessel or organ, or via a body opening. Examples: stents, abscess drainage, EVAR.","w":["IR is procedures.","Image-guided.","Not.","PACS."],"k":"IR = image-guided procedures.","rn":[["","Interventional radiology","Interventional radiology","Image guided procedure; Diagnostic or therapeutic"],["","How does interventional radiology gain access?","How does interventional radiology gain access?","Percutaneous puncture; Or a body opening"]]},{"id":13061,"t":"Interventional Radiology","s":"Put the steps of metallic stent placement in order.","o":["Balloon to narrowing → inflate → flow resumes → stent","Stent to narrowing → inflate → flow resumes → balloon","Balloon to narrowing → stent → inflate → flow resumes","Contrast injected → flow resumes → no stent needed","Balloon inflated outside → passed in → stent"],"e":"Stent steps: a <b>balloon</b> is advanced to the narrowing/obstruction → <b>inflated</b> → <b>blood flow resumes</b> → a <b>stent</b> is inserted to <b>maintain flow</b>. Stents can be placed in almost any vessel and non-vascular tubes.","w":["Balloon first.","Inflate first.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Balloon → inflate → flow → stent.","rn":[["","Metallic stent: first 2 steps?","Metallic stent: first 2 steps?","Balloon to narrowing; Balloon inflated"],["","Metallic stent: last 2 steps?","Metallic stent: last 2 steps?","Blood flow resumes; Stent maintains flow"]]},{"id":13062,"t":"Interventional Radiology","s":"Which procedures are examples of interventional radiology?","o":["Stents, abscess drainage, EVAR, NG tube placement, venous access","Stents, abscess drainage, EVAR, CXR reporting, venous access","Reporting CXRs, archiving on PACS, bone scans","Stents, appendicectomy, EVAR, NG tube placement","Bone scans, PET-CT and MRI"],"e":"IR examples: <b>stent insertion</b>, <b>percutaneous abscess drainage</b>, <b>EVAR</b>, <b>NG feeding tube</b> placement/reinsertion, and tubes for <b>venous access</b> (not an exhaustive list).","w":["Reporting is not IR.","Not.","Surgery.","Not."],"k":"IR examples.","rn":[["","What are 3 IR procedures?","What are 3 IR procedures?","Stent insertion; Percutaneous abscess drainage; Nasogastic Tube insertion"],["","What are the 2 drain/tube techniques?","What are the 2 drain/tube techniques?","NG feeding tube placement; Venous access tubes"]]},{"id":13063,"t":"Interventional Radiology","f":"im50","s":"This bedside film was taken after NG tube insertion; the tube tip is at <b>X</b>. What must happen?","o":["Do not feed; act now – feeding into lung is fatal","Feed slowly while watching for coughing","Leave it in place and recheck the position on tomorrow’s film","Use it for medication but not feed","Accept it if the patient is stable"],"e":"An <b>NG tube in the right lung</b> has <b>fatal consequences if used for feeding</b>; a misplaced tube that is used is a <b>never event</b>. Always confirm the tip is <b>below the diaphragm, in the stomach</b>. The right main bronchus is wider and more vertical, so tubes tend to go there.","w":["Never event.","Act immediately.","Not.","Not."],"k":"NG tube in lung = never event if used.","img":{"src":"13/ngtube","cap":"AP chest X-ray after nasogastric tube insertion."},"rn":[["","Why is an NG tube in the right lung dangerous?","Why is an NG tube in the right lung dangerous?","Fatal if used for feeding"]]},{"id":13064,"t":"The Chest X-ray","s":"The tube tip <b>X</b> lies in the right lung. Name the airway it most likely passed down.","e":"Misplaced NG tubes tend to enter the right lung because the right main bronchus is wider and more vertical. Feeding through a tube in the lung is fatal: do not use it.","k":"Right main bronchus.","img":{"src":"13/ngtube","cap":"AP chest X-ray after nasogastric tube insertion."},"a":["right main bronchus","right bronchus","right main stem bronchus","right mainstem bronchus"],"rn":[["","CXR patient's right: upper structures?","CXR patient's right: upper structures?","Manubrium; Superior vena cava; Right main bronchus"]]},{"id":13065,"t":"The Chest X-ray","s":"Why is a PA erect chest X-ray in the department preferred?","o":["Heart near receptor, good inspiration, less dose to others","Heart far from the receptor (magnified), good inspiration, less dose","Heart near the receptor, but poor inspiration and more dose","Taken lying in bed for comfort","Scapulae always overlie the lungs"],"e":"PA erect: patient <b>stands</b> with chest against the receptor (beam enters the back), <b>avoiding heart magnification</b> (the heart is anterior, closest to the receptor), allowing <b>good inspiration</b>, and <b>reducing dose to others</b>.","w":["Heart is near the receptor on PA.","Good.","Standing.","Out of the way."],"k":"PA erect: accurate heart size, good inspiration.","rn":[["","What are the 3 benefits of a standing PA CXR?","What are the 3 benefits of a standing PA CXR?","Avoids magnification of heart; Allows good inspiration; Reduces dose to others"]]},{"id":13066,"t":"The Chest X-ray","f":"im52","s":"A very unwell patient has an AP sitting chest X-ray at the bedside that shows a large heart. What is the correct interpretation?","o":["Do not diagnose cardiomegaly; the heart is magnified on AP","Diagnose cardiomegaly; AP shows true heart size","Do not diagnose cardiomegaly; PA magnifies the heart","Diagnose heart failure from the large heart","Repeat as a CT before interpreting"],"e":"On an <b>AP</b> film the heart is further from the receptor and looks <b>bigger</b>, which could wrongly suggest cardiomegaly/heart failure. Check whether the film is PA or AP first; bedside films also often have poor inspiration, rotation and scapulae over the lungs.","w":["Magnified.","AP magnifies.","Not.","Not stated."],"k":"No cardiomegaly diagnosis on AP.","rn":[["","Why does it matter if the heart looks magnified?","Why does it matter if the heart looks magnified?","Could wrongly suggest heart problems"]]},{"id":13067,"t":"The Chest X-ray","s":"Which feature belongs to a “bad” AP sitting chest X-ray?","o":["Scapulae over the lungs and the patient rotated or slumped","Scapulae clear of the lungs and good deep inspiration","Accurate heart size and good inspiration","Standing PA view with no rotation","Standard view comparable with previous films"],"e":"Bad (AP sitting by the bed): heart <b>magnified</b>, <b>poor inspiration</b>, <b>scapulae overlie the lungs</b>, patient may be <b>rotated or slumped</b>; done only when too unwell to go to the department.","w":["Good.","Good.","Good.","Good."],"k":"Bad AP film features."},{"id":13068,"t":"The Chest X-ray","s":"Which labelling of A, B, C and D is correct?","o":["A SVC; B right atrium; C aortic arch; D left ventricle","A SVC; B left atrium; C aortic arch; D left ventricle","A IVC; B right atrium; C pulmonary trunk; D left ventricle","A SVC; B right atrium; C pulmonary trunk; D left atrium","A aortic arch; B right ventricle; C SVC; D left ventricle"],"e":"Right side: SVC (A) above the right atrium (B), with the IVC below. Left side: aortic arch/knuckle (C), pulmonary trunk (H), left atrium, left ventricle (D) forming the left heart border.","w":["B forms the right heart border: the right atrium; the left atrium (behind) is on the left.","The IVC enters at the bottom; C sits above the pulmonary trunk (H).","C is the aortic arch (knuckle); D forms the left heart border (left ventricle).","The aortic arch is on the patient’s left; the SVC on the right."],"k":"Right border: SVC + RA. Left border: knuckle, PT, LA, LV.","img":{"src":"13/cxr","cap":"PA chest X-ray with outlines drawn on. Labels removed."},"rn":[["","CXR patient's left: heart structures?","CXR patient's left: heart structures?","Left atrium; Left ventricle"],["","CXR patient's left: upper structures?","CXR patient's left: upper structures?","Aortic arch; Pulmonary trunk; Left main bronchus"]]},{"id":13069,"t":"The Chest X-ray","s":"Which hemidiaphragm is usually higher, and why?","o":["The right, as the liver lies beneath it","The left, as the heart lies on it","The left, as the stomach lies beneath it","The right, as the spleen lies beneath it","They are always level"],"e":"The <b>right hemidiaphragm</b> is usually slightly <b>higher</b> than the left because the <b>liver</b> sits beneath it.","w":["Right.","Right.","Spleen is left.","Not."],"k":"Right hemidiaphragm higher (liver).","rn":[["","What lies under the right vs left diaphragm on CXR?","What lies under the right vs left diaphragm on CXR?","Right: liver; Left: gastric bubble"]]},{"id":13070,"t":"The Chest X-ray","s":"Which midline structures are seen on a chest X-ray?","o":["Manubrium, trachea and carina","Manubrium, aortic knuckle and carina","Trachea, SVC and carina","Spleen and kidneys","Femoral heads"],"e":"Midline structures on the labelled CXR: <b>manubrium</b>, <b>trachea</b>, <b>carina</b>.","w":["Left.","Right.","Not.","Not."],"k":"CXR midline: manubrium, trachea, carina."},{"id":13071,"t":"The Chest X-ray","s":"Name the structure labelled <b>C</b>, seen as a “knuckle” on the patient’s left.","e":"C is the aortic arch, which appears as the aortic knuckle on the patient’s left.","k":"C = aortic arch (knuckle).","img":{"src":"13/cxr","cap":"PA chest X-ray with outlines drawn on. Labels removed."},"a":["aortic arch","aortic knuckle","arch of aorta","arch of the aorta","aorta"],"rn":[["","CXR patient's left: upper structures?","CXR patient's left: upper structures?","Aortic arch; Pulmonary trunk; Left main bronchus"]]},{"id":13072,"t":"Modalities and Radiation","s":"A child is suspected of having a fracture after trauma, and the team wants to avoid unnecessary radiation for soft-tissue assessment. Which modalities avoid ionising radiation?","o":["Ultrasound and MRI","CT and X-ray","Fluoroscopy and PET","Nuclear medicine and CT","X-ray and fluoroscopy"],"e":"Only <b>ultrasound</b> and <b>MRI</b> are non-ionising; others require benefit to outweigh risk.","w":["Ionising.","Ionising.","Ionising.","Ionising."],"k":"Non-ionising options.","rn":[["","Which 2 modalities use no ionising radiation?","Which 2 modalities use no ionising radiation?","Ultrasound; MRI"]]},{"id":13073,"t":"Fluoroscopy","s":"A surgeon needs live X-ray images during an orthopaedic operation in theatre. Which equipment and modality?","o":["C-arm image intensifier – fluoroscopy","C-arm image intensifier – CT","Portable ultrasound – Doppler","Mobile gamma camera – nuclear medicine","Portable MRI scanner"],"e":"The <b>C-arm</b> (image intensifier) gives live X-ray pictures in <b>theatre</b> – <b>fluoroscopy</b>.","w":["Fluoroscopy.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Theatre live X-ray = C-arm.","rn":[["","What are 3 other uses of fluoroscopy?","What are 3 other uses of fluoroscopy?","Ba (barium) examinations; C arm in theatre; Urology procedures"]]},{"id":13074,"t":"MRI","s":"A patient with a pacemaker needs imaging of soft tissue in the spine. What is the main safety concern with MRI?","o":["Some pacemakers are unsafe in the always-on magnet","Some pacemakers are damaged by the radiation dose of MRI","Some pacemakers react with iodinated contrast","Pacemakers block ultrasound waves","Pacemakers cause 2D overlap on the image"],"e":"<b>Cardiac pacemakers (some)</b> are MRI contraindications; anything <b>electronic</b> is dangerous in the always-on magnetic field.","w":["MRI is non-ionising.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Electronic implants and MRI.","rn":[["","What must you remember about the MRI magnet?","What must you remember about the MRI magnet?","It is ALWAYS on!"]]},{"id":13075,"t":"MRI","s":"A patient has a firmly fixed knee replacement and needs an MRI. What should you expect?","o":["MRI can usually proceed, with some artefact","MRI is absolutely contraindicated with any metal implant","MRI will pull the knee replacement out","MRI must be replaced by PET in all cases","MRI requires a quench beforehand"],"e":"<b>Orthopaedic hardware</b> (joint replacements, plates, screws) is <b>not</b> a contraindication, but may <b>degrade the image</b> (artefact) near it.","w":["Hardware is not a contraindication.","Fixed.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Orthopaedic metal: OK, artefact."},{"id":13076,"t":"Nuclear Medicine and PET","s":"This fused image shows a colour “hot” mass in the liver overlying a cross-sectional slice. Which modality produced it?","o":["PET-CT","Plain X-ray","Ultrasound","Fluoroscopy","Standard MRI alone"],"e":"<b>PET-CT</b> combines <b>cross-sectional anatomy (CT)</b> with <b>metabolic information (PET)</b>.","w":["No metabolism.","No metabolism.","No metabolism.","No metabolic tracer."],"k":"PET-CT = anatomy + metabolism.","img":{"src":"13/petct","cap":"Axial fused colour image of the upper abdomen."},"rn":[["","What does PET-CT combine?","What does PET-CT combine?","Cross-sectional anatomic information (CT); Metabolic information (PET)"],["","Why is PET combined with CT/MRI?","Why is PET combined with CT/MRI?","PET images are noisy; To see anatomy easily"]]},{"id":13077,"t":"Nuclear Medicine and PET","s":"Why are PET images usually combined with CT or MRI?","o":["PET is noisy with little anatomy, so CT/MRI locates hot spots","PET is noisy with little function, so CT/MRI adds function","PET gives too much anatomy, so CT simplifies it","CT reduces the radiation dose from PET","MRI makes PET faster to perform"],"e":"PET images are <b>noisy</b>, so they are usually combined with <b>CT/MRI</b> so the anatomy can be seen.","w":["Function from PET.","Little anatomy.","Not.","Not."],"k":"PET needs anatomical fusion.","rn":[["","Why is PET combined with CT/MRI?","Why is PET combined with CT/MRI?","PET images are noisy; To see anatomy easily"]]},{"id":13078,"t":"Contrast Agents","s":"A patient receives a contrast CT and later a gadolinium MRI. Which risk is linked to each?","o":["Iodine: allergy and nephrotoxicity; gadolinium: NSF in renal failure","Iodine: NSF in renal failure; gadolinium: allergy and nephrotoxicity","Iodine and gadolinium: both cause NSF only","Iodine: radiation burns; gadolinium: allergy","Neither carries any significant risk"],"e":"Iodinated contrast: <b>allergic reactions</b>, <b>pregnancy</b> concerns, <b>contrast-induced nephrotoxicity</b>. Gadolinium: <b>NSF</b>, most in poor kidney function.","w":["Swapped.","Different.","Not.","Risks."],"k":"Contrast-specific risks.","rn":[["","Contrast: which 2 kidney risks?","Contrast: which 2 kidney risks?","Contrast induced nephrotoxicity; Poor renal function"]]},{"id":13079,"t":"Contrast Agents","s":"Which statement about contrast in pregnancy is correct?","o":["Avoid if possible; it crosses to the foetus","Avoid if possible, even though it never crosses to the foetus","Give as normal; pregnancy is irrelevant to contrast","Give double the dose for a clearer image","Always cancel imaging in pregnancy"],"e":"<b>Pregnancy</b>: <b>avoid if possible</b> – contrast <b>crosses to the foetus</b>; an alternative may be suitable (radiology decision).","w":["It crosses.","Consider.","Not.","Alternatives may exist."],"k":"Avoid contrast in pregnancy if possible.","rn":[["","Why avoid contrast in pregnancy?","Why avoid contrast in pregnancy?","Crosses to the foetus"]]},{"id":13080,"t":"Radiography","s":"Which modality is cheapest and most readily available with great bone detail, but only 2D?","o":["Plain radiography","CT","MRI","Ultrasound","Nuclear medicine"],"e":"<b>Plain radiography</b>: great spatial (bone) resolution, cheap and readily available; but 2D only.","w":["Cross-sectional.","Expensive.","Non-ionising, poor bone.","Functional."],"k":"Plain X-ray: cheap, 2D.","rn":[["","What are the 3 strengths of radiography?","What are the 3 strengths of radiography?","Great spatial resolution, especially bone; Readily available; Cheap"]]},{"id":13081,"t":"Ultrasound","s":"A patient needs imaging of the gallbladder region but has a lot of bowel gas. Which limitation may affect ultrasound?","o":["Ultrasound cannot see through gas","Ultrasound cannot see through fluid","Ultrasound uses ionising radiation","Ultrasound needs a magnet","Ultrasound is not multiplanar"],"e":"Ultrasound <b>cannot see through bone or gas</b>.","w":["Fluid is fine.","Non-ionising.","Not.","It is."],"k":"Gas blocks ultrasound.","rn":[["","What are the 2 weaknesses of ultrasound?","What are the 2 weaknesses of ultrasound?","Operator-dependent; Can't see bone or gas"]]},{"id":13082,"t":"MRI","s":"Which modality cannot see calcification or gas well?","o":["MRI","CT","Plain X-ray","Fluoroscopy","PET-CT"],"e":"MRI weaknesses include that it <b>cannot see calcification or gas</b>.","w":["Shows calcification.","Shows gas (black).","Shows gas.","CT component shows."],"k":"MRI misses calcification/gas.","rn":[["","MRI weakness: what can't it see?","MRI weakness: what can't it see?","Calcification; Gas"]]},{"id":13083,"t":"Why Imaging Matters","s":"Which pair of milestones were both in 1896?","o":["Edison’s fluoroscopy and the first UK radiology department","Röntgen’s X-rays and the first UK radiology department","Edison’s fluoroscopy and the X-ray tube","Foetal ultrasound and the C-arm","CT and the first MRI scanner"],"e":"<b>1896</b>: <b>fluoroscopy</b> invented by <b>Edison</b>; <b>first UK radiology department</b> opens.","w":["1895.","1913.","1957/1955.","1971/1977."],"k":"1896 milestones.","rn":[["","What 3 places are found in an X-ray department?","What 3 places are found in an X-ray department?","Reporting station (radiologist or radiographer); Interventional radiology; Multidisciplinary team meeting"],["","Why do most patients attending hospital need to go to the X-ray (radiology) department?","Why do most patients attending hospital need to go to the X-ray (radiology) department?","To aid diagnosis; To aid clinical management"]]},{"id":13084,"t":"History of Imaging","s":"In what year was foetal ultrasound introduced?","o":["1957","1955","1950","1971","1913"],"e":"<b>1957</b>: <b>foetal ultrasound</b>.","w":["C-arm.","PET.","CT.","X-ray tube."],"k":"Foetal ultrasound 1957."},{"id":13085,"t":"MRI","s":"A patient has suspected lesions next to the ventricles. Name the sequence shown in <b>B</b>, which suppresses fluid so these stand out.","e":"B is FLAIR: fluid signal is suppressed so lesions beside CSF stand out.","k":"B = FLAIR.","img":{"src":"13/mriseq","cap":"Axial brain MRI, same level, three sequences. Labels removed."},"a":["flair","fluid attenuated inversion recovery","fluid-attenuated inversion recovery"],"rn":[["","What does FLAIR do?","What does FLAIR do?","Suppresses fluid brightness"]]},{"id":13086,"t":"Radiography","s":"Which image would best identify a hidden supracondylar fracture in a child when no fracture line is visible?","o":["A lateral elbow X-ray showing a displaced fat pad","A lateral elbow X-ray showing a fat-fluid level","A bone scan of the upper limb","A PET-CT of the elbow","An ultrasound of the elbow joint"],"e":"A <b>raised/displaced fat pad</b> on an elbow X-ray suggests an <b>effusion</b> and occult fracture (e.g. supracondylar).","w":["Knee sign.","Not stated.","Not stated.","Not stated."],"k":"Fat pad sign."},{"id":13087,"t":"Radiography","s":"What is a hiatus hernia, and how might it appear on a CXR?","o":["Stomach behind the heart: a gas bubble with an air-fluid level","Stomach behind the heart: a dense white mass with no gas","Bowel in the chest: multiple air-fluid levels below the diaphragm","A lung lobe collapse behind the heart","A fluid-filled joint behind the heart"],"e":"A <b>hiatus hernia</b>: part of the <b>stomach sits behind the heart</b>, showing a gas bubble with an <b>air-fluid level</b> behind the heart shadow – easy to miss on a 2D image.","w":["Gas bubble.","Not stated.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Hiatus hernia behind the heart.","rn":[["","CXR: how can you tell a shadow behind the heart is stomach?","CXR: how can you tell a shadow behind the heart is stomach?","Horizontal line; Fluid level of contents; Not a tumour"],["","What does a hiatus hernia show on CXR?","What does a hiatus hernia show on CXR?","Fluid level of stomach contents"],["","Hiatus hernia","Hiatus hernia","Stomach up through diaphragm; Sits behind the heart"]]},{"id":13088,"t":"Radiography","s":"A tissue appears dark grey on a plain X-ray, darker than muscle but lighter than lung. What is it most likely?","o":["Fat","Bone","Air","Metal","Contrast"],"e":"Densities black → white: air, <b>fat (dark grey)</b>, soft tissue/fluid (light grey), bone (white).","w":["White.","Black.","Whiter than bone.","Whiter."],"k":"Fat = dark grey.","rn":[["","What are the 4 tissue densities on a CXR?","What are the 4 tissue densities on a CXR?","Air; Fat; Soft tissue/fluid; Bone"]]},{"id":13089,"t":"Radiography","s":"Which statement about scatter is correct?","o":["Scattered photons are deflected, adding fog and staff dose","Scattered photons are deflected, sharpening the image","Scattered photons pass straight through, making areas black","Scattered photons are absorbed, making bone white","Scatter occurs only in MRI"],"e":"<b>Scattered</b> photons are <b>deflected</b> from their original path, adding <b>fog</b> and <b>dose to staff</b>.","w":["Fog.","Transmission.","Absorption.","X-rays."],"k":"Scatter = fog + staff dose.","rn":[["","Scattered Photons","Scattered Photons","Deflected from original path; Go off at an angle"]]},{"id":13090,"t":"MRI","s":"Which statement about CT and MRI choice is correct?","o":["CT for bone and speed; MRI for soft tissue, slower, needs screening","CT for soft tissue and speed; MRI for bone, slower, needs screening","CT for bone and speed; MRI for soft tissue, faster, no screening","CT is non-ionising; MRI uses radiation","CT and MRI are interchangeable"],"e":"CT: cross-sectional, good bone/spatial resolution, fast, ionising, limited soft-tissue contrast. MRI: exquisite soft tissue, non-ionising, slower (10–45 min), magnet safety.","w":["Swapped.","Slower; screening.","Swapped.","Different."],"k":"CT vs MRI choice.","rn":[["","What are the 3 strengths of MRI?","What are the 3 strengths of MRI?","Non-ionising; Multiplanar; Exquisite soft tissue contrast"],["","What are the 4 tissue densities on a CXR?","What are the 4 tissue densities on a CXR?","Air; Fat; Soft tissue/fluid; Bone"],["","What are the 2 weaknesses of CT?","What are the 2 weaknesses of CT?","Ionising radiation; Limited soft tissue contrast"]]},{"id":13091,"t":"History of Imaging","s":"Which statement about the Hounsfield unit is correct?","o":["The CT density scale, named after CT’s inventor","The MRI field-strength unit, named after its inventor","The X-ray dose unit, named after Röntgen","The ultrasound frequency unit","The contrast concentration unit"],"e":"Hounsfield's name lives on in the <b>Hounsfield unit</b> – the CT density scale. MRI field strength is measured in Tesla.","w":["Tesla.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Hounsfield unit = CT density."},{"id":13092,"t":"MRI","s":"Name the unit of MRI magnetic field strength (clinical range 1.5–3).","e":"Clinical MRI is 1.5–3 tesla, using niobium-titanium cooled by liquid helium at −269 °C.","k":"Tesla.","a":["tesla","t"],"rn":[["","What is the typical MRI magnetic field strength?","What is the typical MRI magnetic field strength?","1.5 to 3 Tesla"]]},{"id":13093,"t":"Nuclear Medicine and PET","s":"A patient's bone scan shows multiple areas of uptake in the spine and ribs. What does this suggest in the context?","o":["Bone metastases, e.g. from prostate cancer","Multiple healing fractures from a fall","Normal uptake in growing bone","Patent fallopian tubes","Contrast nephrotoxicity"],"e":"The slide example: a bone scan of a <b>patient with bone metastases from prostate cancer</b> (anterior and posterior views).","w":["Context.","Not stated.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Bone scan uptake = metastases.","rn":[["","Nuclear medicine bone scan: what can it show?","Nuclear medicine bone scan: what can it show?","Bone metastases; From prostate cancer; Dark hot spots"]]},{"id":13094,"t":"Modalities and Radiation","s":"Which modality would a radiographer most likely use to outline the oesophagus as the patient swallows?","o":["Fluoroscopy with a barium swallow","Fluoroscopy with gadolinium","MRI with gadolinium","Ultrasound with Doppler","PET with FDG"],"e":"A <b>barium swallow</b> under <b>fluoroscopy</b> outlines the oesophagus as the patient swallows.","w":["Barium.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Barium swallow = fluoroscopy.","rn":[["","Give an example of a fluoroscopy procedure.","Give an example of a fluoroscopy procedure.","Barium swallow; Patient swallows liquid barium sulphate"],["","What are the 3 fluoroscopy examples?","What are the 3 fluoroscopy examples?","Barium swallow; Barium enema; Hysterosalpingogram (HSG)"],["","What does a barium swallow look for?","What does a barium swallow look for?","The swallowing mechanism; Anything restricting the oesophagus"]]},{"id":13095,"t":"History of Imaging","s":"Which pairing of history and inventor is correct?","o":["CT – Godfrey Hounsfield","X-rays – Edison","Fluoroscopy – Röntgen","CT – Röntgen","MRI – Hounsfield"],"e":"<b>Röntgen</b>: X-rays (1895); <b>Edison</b>: fluoroscopy (1896); <b>Hounsfield</b>: CT (1971).","w":["Röntgen.","Edison.","Hounsfield.","CT."],"k":"Inventors."},{"id":13096,"t":"Ultrasound","s":"A clinician wants dynamic, real-time imaging of a moving joint without radiation. Which modality fits best?","o":["Ultrasound","CT","Nuclear medicine","Plain X-ray","PET-CT"],"e":"Ultrasound is <b>dynamic</b> (real time), <b>multiplanar</b> and <b>non-ionising</b>.","w":["Ionising, static.","Functional.","Static.","Not dynamic."],"k":"Real-time non-ionising = ultrasound.","rn":[["","What are the 4 strengths of ultrasound?","What are the 4 strengths of ultrasound?","Non-ionising radiation; Multiplanar; Dynamic; Doppler"]]},{"id":13097,"t":"The Chest X-ray","s":"Which statement about why the heart is magnified on AP films is correct?","o":["The heart is anterior, so further from the receptor on AP","The heart is posterior, so further from the receptor on AP","The heart is anterior, so nearer the receptor on AP","AP uses more radiation, enlarging the heart","AP is taken in expiration, enlarging the heart"],"e":"Because the heart sits at the front of the chest, a PA film puts it close to the receptor (not magnified); on <b>AP</b> it is <b>further away</b> and looks <b>bigger</b>.","w":["Anterior.","Further.","Not stated.","Not the reason."],"k":"Distance from receptor → magnification.","rn":[["","Why does PA avoid magnifying the heart?","Why does PA avoid magnifying the heart?","Heart near chest front; So near the receptor"]]},{"id":13098,"t":"Computed Tomography","s":"Which modality would most likely be used for surgical planning of a complex tibial plateau fracture?","o":["CT with reconstructions","MRI with gadolinium contrast","Plain AP knee X-ray only","Ultrasound with Doppler","Nuclear medicine bone scan"],"e":"A <b>coronal CT of the knee</b> was shown for <b>surgical planning</b> of complex fractures; CT data can be reconstructed in any plane.","w":["CT was shown for planning.","2D.","Not.","Not."],"k":"CT for fracture planning."},{"id":13099,"t":"Fluoroscopy","s":"These images were taken in real time as the patient swallowed contrast. Name the modality.","e":"Fluoroscopy: real-time X-ray, used for barium studies, angiography, stents, the C-arm in theatre and urology.","k":"Fluoroscopy.","img":{"src":"13/fluoro","cap":"Contrast images of the upper gut."},"a":["fluoroscopy","barium swallow","barium study","barium meal","screening"],"rn":[["","Fluoroscopy","Fluoroscopy","Real time moving structures; Continuous X-rays; A series of images"],["","What are 3 other uses of fluoroscopy?","What are 3 other uses of fluoroscopy?","Ba (barium) examinations; C arm in theatre; Urology procedures"]]},{"id":13100,"t":"The Chest X-ray","s":"Name the vessel labelled <b>A</b>, forming the upper right mediastinal border.","e":"A is the superior vena cava, above the right atrium on the patient’s right.","k":"A = SVC.","img":{"src":"13/cxr","cap":"PA chest X-ray with outlines drawn on. Labels removed."},"a":["superior vena cava","svc","superior vena cava svc"],"rn":[["","CXR patient's right: upper structures?","CXR patient's right: upper structures?","Manubrium; Superior vena cava; Right main bronchus"]]},{"id":13101,"t":"Interventional Radiology","s":"Which statement about ultrasound history is correct?","o":["Therapeutic use in the 1930s; diagnostic from the 1950s","Diagnostic use in the 1930s; therapeutic from the 1950s","Invented in 1895 with X-rays","Introduced with CT in 1971","Never used therapeutically"],"e":"Ultrasound was first developed for <b>therapeutic</b> purposes in the <b>1930s</b>; used for <b>diagnosis</b> from the <b>1950s</b>. A 1977 scanner photo showed how large early machines were.","w":["Swapped.","Not.","Not.","It was."],"k":"Ultrasound history.","rn":[["","Interventional radiology","Interventional radiology","Image guided procedure; Diagnostic or therapeutic"]]},{"id":13102,"t":"The Chest X-ray","s":"Name the heart chamber labelled <b>B</b>, which forms the right heart border.","e":"B is the right atrium. Right-sided structures: SVC, right atrium, horizontal fissure, IVC.","k":"B = right atrium.","img":{"src":"13/cxr","cap":"PA chest X-ray with outlines drawn on. Labels removed."},"a":["right atrium","ra","right atrium ra"],"rn":[["","CXR patient's right: middle structures?","CXR patient's right: middle structures?","Horizontal fissure; Right atrium; Oblique fissure"]]},{"id":13103,"t":"The Chest X-ray","s":"Name the line labelled <b>E</b>, seen only in the right lung.","e":"E is the horizontal fissure, separating the right upper and middle lobes. The right lung has 3 lobes, the left 2 (the heart takes space on the left); both have an oblique fissure.","k":"E = horizontal fissure.","img":{"src":"13/cxr","cap":"PA chest X-ray with outlines drawn on. Labels removed."},"a":["horizontal fissure","horizontal","transverse fissure"],"rn":[["","Which fissure is only on the right?","Which fissure is only on the right?","Horizontal fissure"],["","CXR patient's right: middle structures?","CXR patient's right: middle structures?","Horizontal fissure; Right atrium; Oblique fissure"]]},{"id":13104,"t":"The Chest X-ray","s":"A patient is rotated and slumped on a bedside film with scapulae over the lungs. What is the best next step if they become well enough?","o":["Repeat as a PA erect film in the department","Repeat as an AP supine film at the bedside","Repeat as a CT immediately","Diagnose cardiomegaly from the current film","Perform an MRI of the chest"],"e":"Best results are in the <b>X-ray department</b> with a <b>PA erect</b> film; bedside AP films are only when too unwell.","w":["PA erect.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"PA erect when possible."},{"id":13105,"t":"Contrast Agents","s":"Which statement about early radiology and today's practice is correct?","o":["Early workers lacked protection; benefit must outweigh risk","Early workers were well protected; today the risk is ignored","Radiation was always well understood","Ionising radiation is now known to be harmless","Only MRI uses ionising radiation today"],"e":"Early workers suffered radiation effects; today ionising examinations require that <b>benefit outweighs risk</b>.","w":["No protection then.","Not.","Harmful.","Non-ionising."],"k":"Benefit must outweigh risk.","rn":[["","Poor renal function: is contrast still used?","Poor renal function: is contrast still used?","Risk/benefit assessment; Go ahead if benefit outweighs"],["","Does ionising radiation only have risks?","Does ionising radiation only have risks?","No; Risks as well as benefits"]]},{"id":13106,"t":"Nuclear Medicine and PET","s":"Which modality has the main weakness of very limited anatomical information?","o":["Nuclear medicine","Plain X-ray","CT","MRI","Ultrasound"],"e":"Nuclear medicine gives <b>functional</b> information but <b>very limited anatomy</b>.","w":["Bone detail.","Anatomy.","Anatomy.","Anatomy."],"k":"NM: limited anatomy.","rn":[["","What is the strength of nuclear medicine?","What is the strength of nuclear medicine?","Functional information"],["","What are the 2 weaknesses of nuclear medicine?","What are the 2 weaknesses of nuclear medicine?","Very limited anatomical information; Radiation to patient/staff/population"],["","Which 2 other modalities use ionising radiation?","Which 2 other modalities use ionising radiation?","Nuclear medicine; PET"]]},{"id":13107,"t":"MRI","s":"Which statement about CT scan time history is correct?","o":["Once 5 minutes per slice; now whole scans in minutes","Once 5 seconds per slice; now hours per scan","Always instantaneous since 1971","Once 5 hours per scan; now 5 minutes per slice","Unchanged since its invention"],"e":"Scans that once took <b>5 minutes per slice</b> are now completed in <b>minutes</b> (whole scan).","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Changed."],"k":"CT speed improvements.","rn":[["","How long can other MRI scans take?","How long can other MRI scans take?","Up to about 45 minutes"],["","How long does an MRI head scan take?","How long does an MRI head scan take?","About 10 to 15 minutes"]]},{"id":13108,"t":"Contrast Agents","s":"A patient with poor renal function needs MRI with contrast. What risk is relevant?","o":["Nephrogenic systemic fibrosis from gadolinium","Contrast-induced nephrotoxicity from iodine","Allergic reaction to barium","Radiation injury to the kidneys","Lipohaemarthrosis"],"e":"<b>NSF</b> relates to <b>gadolinium</b> (MRI contrast); patients with <b>poor kidney function</b> are most at risk.","w":["MRI uses gadolinium.","Not.","Non-ionising.","Not."],"k":"Gadolinium + renal impairment → NSF.","rn":[["","Nephrogenic systemic fibrosis","Nephrogenic systemic fibrosis","Related to Gd (gadolinium) contrast"]]},{"id":13109,"t":"Contrast Agents","s":"Which statement best describes how NSF presents?","o":["Firm skin patches with oedema over weeks, sometimes lungs and heart","Instant anaphylaxis within minutes of injection","Firm skin patches appearing within hours, never other organs","Bone fractures developing over weeks","Hearing loss after the scan"],"e":"<b>NSF</b> mainly affects <b>skin</b> (firm patches with oedema), can affect lungs, heart, muscles, and <b>develops over weeks</b>.","w":["Weeks.","Weeks; other organs.","Not.","Not."],"k":"NSF presentation.","rn":[["","What does the skin develop in NSF?","What does the skin develop in NSF?","Firm patches with oedema"]]},{"id":13110,"t":"The Chest X-ray","s":"Name the region labelled <b>F</b>, where the left hemidiaphragm meets the chest wall.","e":"F is the left costophrenic angle.","k":"F = costophrenic angle.","img":{"src":"13/cxr","cap":"PA chest X-ray with outlines drawn on. Labels removed."},"a":["left costophrenic angle","costophrenic angle","costophrenic","left costo-phrenic angle","costo-phrenic angle"],"rn":[["","CXR patient's left: lower structures?","CXR patient's left: lower structures?","Oblique fissure; Diaphragm; Left costophrenic angle"],["","Which angle lies at the lung base?","Which angle lies at the lung base?","Costophrenic angle"]]},{"id":13111,"t":"PACS and Which Modality","s":"A patient's hip MRI is compared with a CT of the same region. Which difference is expected?","o":["MRI shows muscle better; CT shows bone detail","MRI shows bone detail better; CT shows muscle better","MRI and CT look the same","MRI shows bone whitest","CT shows no bone"],"e":"In the “which modality?” pelvis images: <b>MRI</b> – soft tissue and muscle well defined; <b>CT</b> – bone detail, less muscle definition; plain X-ray – 2D, bone white.","w":["Swapped.","Different.","Bone dark on MRI.","Bone detail."],"k":"MRI vs CT on the same region.","rn":[["","CT vs MRI for muscle","CT vs MRI for muscle","CT: seen, can't define; MRI: defines much better"]]},{"id":13112,"t":"Radiography","s":"Which statement best summarises why radiographers collimate and filter the beam?","o":["To reduce patient dose and improve image quality","To increase dose and improve image quality","To reduce dose but worsen image quality","To magnify the heart","To heat the tube"],"e":"<b>Filtration</b> removes useless low-energy photons; <b>collimation</b> restricts the field – both <b>reduce dose</b> and improve the image.","w":["Reduce.","Improves.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Dose reduction techniques."},{"id":13113,"t":"Ultrasound","s":"Which statement about Doppler and colour on ultrasound is correct?","o":["Colour Doppler shows blood flow","Colour Doppler shows bone density","Colour Doppler shows FDG uptake","Colour Doppler shows tissue fat","Colour is never used in ultrasound images"],"e":"<b>Doppler</b> ultrasound shows <b>blood flow</b> in <b>colour</b>.","w":["Not.","PET.","Not.","It is."],"k":"Colour Doppler = flow.","rn":[["","Which ultrasound type shows blood flow?","Which ultrasound type shows blood flow?","Doppler"],["","Doppler","Doppler","Shows blood flow; Through vessels"]]},{"id":13114,"t":"History of Imaging","s":"Which statement about the first UK radiology department and image intensifier is correct?","o":["First UK department 1896; C-arm 1955","First UK department 1895; C-arm 1957","First UK department 1913; C-arm 1955","First UK department 1896; C-arm 1977","First UK department 1955; C-arm 1896"],"e":"<b>1896</b>: first UK radiology department. <b>1955</b>: image intensifier (C-arm) in use.","w":["1896; 1955.","1896.","1955.","Swapped."],"k":"1896 dept; 1955 C-arm."},{"id":13115,"t":"Nuclear Medicine and PET","s":"Which pairing of PET fact and explanation is correct?","o":["Expensive and relatively few","Cheap and widely available in most hospitals","Non-ionising and quick","Detailed anatomy without CT","Uses barium as its tracer"],"e":"PET scanners are <b>expensive</b> and there are <b>relatively few</b> of them (more in London).","w":["Expensive.","Ionising.","Noisy.","FDG."],"k":"PET availability limited.","rn":[["","Where are most PET scanners?","Where are most PET scanners?","More in London; Few and far between; Very expensive"]]},{"id":13116,"t":"MRI","s":"Which of the following explains the new development of “almost helium-free MRI”?","o":["To address the scarcity of helium","To reduce the X-ray dose","To make the magnet switch off at night","To replace gadolinium contrast","To speed up CT"],"e":"<b>Almost helium-free MRI</b> addresses the <b>scarcity of helium</b>, which cools the superconducting magnet.","w":["Not.","Always on.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Helium scarcity.","rn":[["","What is the new development in MRI magnets?","What is the new development in MRI magnets?","Almost helium free MRI; Addresses the scarcity of helium"]]},{"id":13117,"t":"MRI","s":"Which statement about the RF coil in MRI is correct?","o":["It emits radio-frequency pulses","It emits X-rays into the patient","It cools the superconducting magnet","It detects gamma rays from tracers","It emits ultrasound pulses"],"e":"The <b>RF coil</b> emits short-wave <b>radio-frequency pulses</b> into the patient, flipping protons.","w":["Not.","Helium cools it.","Gamma camera.","Transducer."],"k":"RF coil emits RF pulses.","rn":[["","What does the radio-frequency coil do?","What does the radio-frequency coil do?","Emits short wave RF pulses; Into the patient"]]},{"id":13118,"t":"Interventional Radiology","s":"A clinician confirms an NG tube tip lies below the diaphragm in the stomach. What does this mean?","o":["Correctly positioned; feeding can proceed","Correctly positioned; but feeding must wait a day","In the right lung; do not feed","In the trachea; remove it","A never event has occurred"],"e":"Always confirm the NG tube tip is <b>below the diaphragm, in the stomach</b> before feeding – this is correct positioning.","w":["Proceed.","Below diaphragm.","Not.","Correct position."],"k":"NG tip below diaphragm = correct."},{"id":13119,"t":"The Chest X-ray","s":"Name the gas-filled structure labelled <b>G</b>, under the left hemidiaphragm.","e":"G is the gastric bubble (gas in the stomach). The right hemidiaphragm is usually higher because the liver lies beneath it.","k":"G = gastric bubble.","img":{"src":"13/cxr","cap":"PA chest X-ray with outlines drawn on. Labels removed."},"a":["gastric bubble","stomach","gastric air bubble","stomach bubble","fundus"],"rn":[["","What lies under the right vs left diaphragm on CXR?","What lies under the right vs left diaphragm on CXR?","Right: liver; Left: gastric bubble"]]},{"id":13120,"t":"The Chest X-ray","s":"Name the vessel labelled <b>H</b>, just below the aortic knuckle on the left.","e":"H is the pulmonary trunk, between the aortic knuckle above and the left atrium below.","k":"H = pulmonary trunk.","img":{"src":"13/cxr","cap":"PA chest X-ray with outlines drawn on. Labels removed."},"a":["pulmonary trunk","pulmonary artery","main pulmonary artery","pulmonary"],"rn":[["","CXR patient's left: upper structures?","CXR patient's left: upper structures?","Aortic arch; Pulmonary trunk; Left main bronchus"]]},{"id":13121,"t":"Ultrasound","s":"A 28-year-old woman who is 20 weeks pregnant has pain in the right upper abdomen. The team wants to look at her liver and gallbladder in real time and avoid any ionising radiation to the foetus. They also want to see blood flow in a vessel near the liver.<br><br>Which modality is most suitable, and what is one of its limitations?","o":["Ultrasound; it is operator-dependent and cannot see through bone or gas","CT; it gives a relatively high radiation dose","Plain X-ray; it is 2D only","Nuclear medicine; it gives very limited anatomical information","MRI with gadolinium; the magnet is always on and gadolinium crosses easily to the foetus during pregnancy"],"e":"Step 1: ultrasound uses sound waves (non-ionising), is multiplanar and dynamic (real time), and Doppler shows blood flow, so it fits all her needs. Step 2: its weaknesses are that it is operator-dependent and cannot see through bone or gas.","w":["CT uses ionising radiation, which they want to avoid.","Plain X-ray uses ionising radiation and is not real time.","Nuclear medicine uses radioactive tracers.","Ultrasound meets her needs without a magnet or contrast."],"k":"Pregnancy + real time + flow → ultrasound (operator-dependent; no bone/gas).","rn":[["","What are the 2 weaknesses of ultrasound?","What are the 2 weaknesses of ultrasound?","Operator-dependent; Can't see bone or gas"]]},{"id":13122,"t":"MRI","s":"A 60-year-old man is booked for an MRI of his brain. On the safety questionnaire he reports a hip replacement 5 years ago and an implanted cardiac defibrillator. He also worked as a metal grinder and once had a fragment of metal in his eye. The radiographer reviews the form before he enters the scanner room.<br><br>Which item(s) are contraindications, and why is the questionnaire done even when the scanner is not in use?","o":["The defibrillator and eye metal; the magnet is always on","The hip replacement only; the magnet is switched off overnight","All three; MRI uses ionising radiation","None; MRI is safe for all metal","The hip replacement and defibrillator; orthopaedic hardware is the main danger in an MRI scanner"],"e":"Step 1: MRI contraindications include implanted cardiac defibrillators and metal fragments in the eye, but not orthopaedic hardware such as joint replacements, plates and screws, which are usually fine. Step 2: the magnet is always on and cannot simply be switched off, so screening is needed before anyone enters the room.","w":["Orthopaedic hardware is usually fine, and the magnet is never switched off.","MRI is non-ionising, and the hip replacement is usually fine.","Some implants and metal are contraindications.","Orthopaedic hardware is not a contraindication."],"k":"MRI: defibrillator and eye metal contraindicated; joint replacement OK; magnet always on.","rn":[["","What must you remember about the MRI magnet?","What must you remember about the MRI magnet?","It is ALWAYS on!"],["","Which 3 other items are MRI contraindications?","Which 3 other items are MRI contraindications?","Bone growth/fusion stimulator; Cochlear/ear implant; Metal fragments in eye"],["","Which patients may have metal fragments in the eye?","Which patients may have metal fragments in the eye?","Grinding or welding injuries"]]},{"id":13123,"t":"Contrast Agents","s":"A 75-year-old woman with poor kidney function needs a CT scan with iodinated contrast. She had a rash after a previous contrast scan. Six months later she has an MRI with gadolinium, and over the following weeks develops firm, swollen patches on her skin.<br><br>Which checks were needed before the CT, and what is the most likely cause of her skin change?","o":["Allergy history and renal function; nephrogenic systemic fibrosis from gadolinium","Only pregnancy status; an allergic reaction to iodine","Renal function only; contrast-induced nephrotoxicity in the skin","No checks needed; ultrasound gel reaction","Allergy history only; nephrogenic systemic fibrosis caused by iodinated CT contrast"],"e":"Step 1: before contrast, the team must ask about allergic reactions, check pregnancy status and establish renal function because of contrast-induced nephrotoxicity, using a risk/benefit decision if kidney function is poor. Step 2: nephrogenic systemic fibrosis (NSF) is related to gadolinium (MRI contrast), mainly affects the skin with firm patches and oedema, develops over weeks, and is most likely in patients with poor kidney function.","w":["Her allergy history and kidney function also matter, and the skin change developed after gadolinium.","Nephrotoxicity affects the kidneys, not the skin, and allergy history is also needed.","Checks are needed before contrast.","NSF is linked to gadolinium, not iodine."],"k":"Before contrast: allergy, pregnancy, renal function; NSF = gadolinium + poor kidneys.","rn":[["","Nephrogenic systemic fibrosis","Nephrogenic systemic fibrosis","Related to Gd (gadolinium) contrast"]]},{"id":13124,"t":"The Chest X-ray","s":"A 70-year-old man is very unwell on the ward and has a chest X-ray taken sitting up in bed. The film shows a large heart shadow, poor inspiration and the scapulae overlying the lungs. A junior doctor wants to diagnose an enlarged heart (cardiomegaly).<br><br>What type of film is this, and why should the junior doctor be cautious?","o":["AP; the heart is magnified, so cardiomegaly should not be diagnosed","PA; the heart size is accurate, so cardiomegaly is confirmed","AP; the heart appears smaller than it really is","PA; the scapulae always overlie the lungs on PA films","Lateral; the heart looks bigger because the X-ray beam passes from the side"],"e":"Step 1: a film taken sitting by the bed with poor inspiration and the scapulae over the lungs is an AP (anteroposterior) film, done only when the patient is too unwell to go to the department. Step 2: on an AP film the heart is magnified (the heart is anterior, further from the receptor), so cardiomegaly should not be diagnosed; a PA erect film gives an accurate heart size.","w":["These are features of an AP film, where heart size is unreliable.","On an AP film the heart is magnified, not smaller.","On a PA film the scapulae are out of the lung fields.","The features described are those of an AP film."],"k":"AP film magnifies the heart: don't diagnose cardiomegaly.","rn":[["","Why does it matter if the heart looks magnified?","Why does it matter if the heart looks magnified?","Could wrongly suggest heart problems"]]},{"id":13125,"t":"Radiography","s":"A medical student looks at a chest X-ray. She sees black areas over the lungs and trachea, white ribs and spine, and a light grey heart shadow. Just under the left diaphragm is a black bubble. She asks why tissues appear in these different shades.<br><br>Which explanation of the shades is correct?","o":["Air transmits photons (black); bone absorbs them (white); soft tissue is light grey","Bone transmits photons (black); air absorbs them (white)","Scattered photons make the bone white","Fat is whiter than bone because it absorbs the most","All tissues absorb equally, and the shades come from the cathode heating the tissues by different amounts"],"e":"Step 1: photons that pass straight through without interacting (transmitted) make black areas, as with air in the lungs, trachea and stomach bubble, while absorbed photons do not reach the receptor and make white areas, as with bone. Step 2: the four densities from black to white are air, fat (dark grey), soft tissue/fluid (light grey, e.g. heart) and bone (white).","w":["The relationship is reversed.","Scattered photons add fog and staff dose, not white bone.","Fat is dark grey, much darker than bone.","Tissues absorb differently, which creates the densities."],"k":"Transmitted → black (air); absorbed → white (bone).","rn":[["","What are the 4 tissue densities on a CXR?","What are the 4 tissue densities on a CXR?","Air; Fat; Soft tissue/fluid; Bone"]]},{"id":13126,"t":"Radiography","s":"A 30-year-old woman falls onto her outstretched arm. Her elbow X-ray shows no clear fracture line, but the fat pad is raised. A week later, her brother injures his knee, and a horizontal X-ray shows a fat–fluid level in the joint.<br><br>What do these two findings most likely indicate?","o":["Both suggest fractures: an occult elbow fracture and an intra-articular knee fracture","Both are normal findings that can be ignored","A soft-tissue sprain at the elbow and an infection of the knee","An elbow dislocation and a knee effusion caused by the normal fluid of the joint","Bone metastases in both joints"],"e":"Step 1: a raised elbow fat pad indicates an occult (hidden) fracture even when no line is seen. Step 2: a fat–fluid level in a joint (lipohaemarthrosis) means an intra-articular fracture until proven otherwise.","w":["These are important signs of fracture.","A raised fat pad suggests occult fracture, and lipohaemarthrosis suggests fracture.","The fat–fluid level indicates fracture, not a simple effusion.","Bone metastases are shown on nuclear medicine bone scans, not by these signs."],"k":"Raised elbow fat pad = occult fracture; lipohaemarthrosis = intra-articular fracture."},{"id":13127,"t":"Nuclear Medicine and PET","s":"A 62-year-old smoker has a mass in his lung on CT. To stage the disease, he is injected with a radioactive glucose-like drug and scanned. The images show bright \"hot spots\" in the lung mass and in both adrenal glands, laid over CT anatomy.<br><br>Which investigation is this, and why do the tumours light up?","o":["PET-CT; FDG accumulates in tumour cells, which need glucose to grow","Ultrasound with Doppler; tumours have increased blood flow","MRI with gadolinium; tumours take up contrast","Plain X-ray; tumours absorb more photons","Nuclear medicine bone scan; tumours are rich in calcium and show the tracer bound to bone"],"e":"Step 1: PET-CT combines cross-sectional anatomy (CT) with metabolic information (PET), after injection of fluorodeoxyglucose (FDG), a radioactive drug similar to glucose. Step 2: cancer cells need glucose to grow, so FDG accumulates in tumour cells and is detected as hot spots; here it shows adrenal metastases, staging the probable lung cancer.","w":["Ultrasound does not use a radioactive glucose-like tracer.","MRI with gadolinium does not produce hot spots from glucose uptake.","Plain X-ray does not show metabolic hot spots.","FDG is a glucose analogue, not a bone tracer."],"k":"PET-CT: FDG (glucose analogue) → tumour hot spots.","rn":[["","What do cancer cells need to grow?","What do cancer cells need to grow?","Glucose"],["","What does FDG do in tumour cells?","What does FDG do in tumour cells?","Accumulates and concentrates; Detected and quantified"]]},{"id":13128,"t":"Interventional Radiology","s":"A 78-year-old woman who cannot swallow after a stroke has a nasogastric (NG) tube placed for feeding. A chest X-ray is taken before feeding starts. The tube tip is seen above the diaphragm, in the right lower lung field. The nurse is ready to start the feed.<br><br>What is the correct action, and why?","o":["Do not feed; a tube in the lung used for feeding is fatal, a never event","Start the feed; any position above the diaphragm is safe","Start the feed slowly to test the position","Do not feed; the tube tip should be in the trachea, so pull it back up into the windpipe","Start the feed; the X-ray cannot show the tube position"],"e":"Step 1: the NG tube tip should be below the diaphragm, in the stomach, so a tip above the diaphragm in the right lung field is misplaced. Step 2: an NG tube in the lung used for feeding has fatal consequences and is a \"never event\", so the position must be confirmed before any feeding.","w":["A tip above the diaphragm is in the wrong place.","Feeding into the lung even slowly is dangerous.","The tip should be in the stomach, not the trachea.","The X-ray does show the tube position."],"k":"NG tip must be below the diaphragm; lung feeding = never event.","rn":[["","Example of a never event?","Example of a never event?","Feeding via NG tube; In the lung"],["","Why is an NG tube in the right lung dangerous?","Why is an NG tube in the right lung dangerous?","Fatal if used for feeding"]]},{"id":13129,"t":"MRI","s":"A 35-year-old woman with suspected inflammation in her brain has an MRI. On one sequence the cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) is dark and the anatomy is very clear. On a second sequence the CSF is bright and an area of oedema is also bright. On a third, the CSF is dark but the area of oedema next to the ventricles stands out clearly.<br><br>Which sequences are these?","o":["1: T1; 2: T2; 3: FLAIR","1: T2; 2: T1; 3: FLAIR","1: FLAIR; 2: T1; 3: T2","1: T1; 2: FLAIR; 3: T2","All three are T1 images taken at different strengths of magnetic field, from 1.5 to 3 Tesla"],"e":"Step 1: on T1 fluid is dark and anatomy is clear, while on T2 fluid is bright, which is good for pathology such as oedema. Step 2: FLAIR is a T2-type image with the fluid signal removed, so CSF is dark and lesions next to CSF stand out.","w":["On T2, fluid is bright, so image 1 is not T2.","FLAIR suppresses fluid but is not the clearest anatomy image here; fluid is bright on T2.","On FLAIR, CSF is dark, so image 2 is not FLAIR.","The appearances differ by sequence, not field strength."],"k":"T1 fluid dark (anatomy); T2 fluid bright (pathology); FLAIR fluid suppressed.","rn":[["","What are the 3 brain MRI sequences compared?","What are the 3 brain MRI sequences compared?","T1; FLAIR; T2"],["","What does FLAIR do?","What does FLAIR do?","Suppresses fluid brightness"]]},{"id":13130,"t":"Modalities and Radiation","s":"A student is shown four images. Image 1 is a slice with very white bone, dark fat and fairly uniform grey soft tissue. Image 2 is a slice with excellent soft-tissue detail and black cortical bone. Image 3 is a fan-shaped grainy grey image. Image 4 is a whole-body picture with blotchy dark uptake and little anatomy.<br><br>Which modalities are these?","o":["1: CT; 2: MRI; 3: ultrasound; 4: nuclear medicine","1: MRI; 2: CT; 3: fluoroscopy; 4: PET-CT","1: plain X-ray; 2: MRI; 3: nuclear medicine; 4: ultrasound","1: CT; 2: ultrasound; 3: MRI; 4: plain X-ray","1: fluoroscopy; 2: CT; 3: ultrasound; 4: MRI, which shows the whole body at once"],"e":"Step 1: a slice with very white bone, dark fat and uniform grey soft tissue is CT, while a slice with excellent soft-tissue detail and black cortical bone is MRI. Step 2: a fan-shaped grainy grey image is ultrasound, and a whole-body image with blotchy uptake and little anatomy is nuclear medicine.","w":["Very white bone with uniform soft tissue is CT, and image 4 has no CT overlay.","Image 1 is a slice, not a 2D overlapping shadow.","Excellent soft-tissue detail with black bone is MRI.","Fluoroscopy is a live series, and image 4 is nuclear medicine."],"k":"CT: white bone slice; MRI: soft tissue, black cortex; US: fan; NM: blotchy whole body.","rn":[["","What are 2 other ways of imaging the body?","What are 2 other ways of imaging the body?","Ultrasound; Nuclear medicine scan"],["","Which 2 modalities use radioactive tracers?","Which 2 modalities use radioactive tracers?","Nuclear medicine; PET CT/MRI"]]},{"id":13501,"t":"The Chest X-ray","s":"Name the midline air-filled tube labelled <b>A</b>.","a":["trachea"],"e":"A is the trachea, a midline structure with the manubrium and carina.","k":"A = trachea.","img":{"src":"13/o_cxr","cap":"Frontal (PA) chest radiograph. Image: Stillwaterising, CC0, Wikimedia Commons."}},{"id":13502,"t":"The Chest X-ray","s":"Name the rounded bulge labelled <b>B</b> on the patient’s left of the upper mediastinum.","a":["aortic knuckle","aortic arch","arch of aorta","aorta"],"e":"B is the aortic knuckle (the aortic arch).","k":"B = aortic knuckle.","img":{"src":"13/o_cxr","cap":"Frontal (PA) chest radiograph. Image: Stillwaterising, CC0, Wikimedia Commons."},"rn":[["","CXR patient's left: upper structures?","CXR patient's left: upper structures?","Aortic arch; Pulmonary trunk; Left main bronchus"]]},{"id":13503,"t":"The Chest X-ray","s":"The dome labelled <b>C</b> sits higher than the other side. What is it and why is it higher?","o":["The right hemidiaphragm, with the liver beneath it","The left hemidiaphragm, with the heart on it","The left hemidiaphragm, with the stomach beneath it","The right hemidiaphragm, with the spleen beneath it","The right hemidiaphragm, raised by a pleural effusion"],"w":["The film is viewed as if facing the patient: C is the patient’s right.","C is on the patient’s right.","The spleen is on the left.","The angle is sharp and the lung clear: normal."],"e":"The right hemidiaphragm (C) is usually higher because the liver lies beneath it. The gastric bubble (F) lies under the left dome.","k":"Right dome higher: liver.","img":{"src":"13/o_cxr","cap":"Frontal (PA) chest radiograph. Image: Stillwaterising, CC0, Wikimedia Commons."},"rn":[["","What lies under the right vs left diaphragm on CXR?","What lies under the right vs left diaphragm on CXR?","Right: liver; Left: gastric bubble"]]},{"id":13504,"t":"The Chest X-ray","s":"On a later film, angle <b>D</b> is lost and the lower zone on that side is uniformly white. What is the most appropriate general term before further tests?","o":["Pleural effusion","Pneumothorax","Hiatus hernia","Dextrocardia","Normal variant"],"w":["A pneumothorax is air: black, not white.","A hiatus hernia is a gas bubble behind the heart.","Dextrocardia is the heart on the right.","A blunted angle with white lower zone is abnormal."],"e":"D is the left costophrenic angle. Blunting with a white lower zone suggests fluid between visceral and parietal pleura: a pleural effusion.","k":"Blunted angle = effusion.","img":{"src":"13/o_cxr","cap":"Frontal (PA) chest radiograph. Image: Stillwaterising, CC0, Wikimedia Commons."}},{"id":13505,"t":"The Chest X-ray","s":"Name the gas lucency labelled <b>F</b>, under the left hemidiaphragm.","a":["gastric bubble","stomach","gastric air bubble","fundus","stomach bubble"],"e":"F is the gastric bubble (air in the fundus of the stomach).","k":"F = gastric bubble.","img":{"src":"13/o_cxr","cap":"Frontal (PA) chest radiograph. Image: Stillwaterising, CC0, Wikimedia Commons."},"rn":[["","What lies under the right vs left diaphragm on CXR?","What lies under the right vs left diaphragm on CXR?","Right: liver; Left: gastric bubble"]]},{"id":13506,"t":"The Chest X-ray","s":"Name the heart chamber forming the right heart border, labelled <b>E</b>.","a":["right atrium","ra"],"e":"E is the right atrium, below the SVC on the patient’s right.","k":"E = right atrium.","img":{"src":"13/o_cxr","cap":"Frontal (PA) chest radiograph. Image: Stillwaterising, CC0, Wikimedia Commons."},"rn":[["","CXR patient's right: middle structures?","CXR patient's right: middle structures?","Horizontal fissure; Right atrium; Oblique fissure"]]}]);
